house, then he's one of the two, the other being his brother William - Dr. Alec. But since William was the victim, as I said, Hamnett must have been the one who killed his own brother!\" [2438] \"Damn!\" Sam said. [2439] \"No, no, Percy,\" Roarke jumped up, \"it's -\"\n[2440] \"Wait, Gordon,\" Reine quietly said, \"Percy, what makes you think Dr. Hamnett Sedra is the main character in this case?\" [2441] Percy glared angrily at Rowe. \"I have several reasons why Dr. Hamnett Sedgwick would want to get his hands on Shakespeare's manuscript. First, he is a bibliophile, and he admits that William told him everything about the manuscript. I dare say he is a typical bookworm and would not let the opportunity to see Shakespeare's true work slip away. Another suspicious point is that he suddenly gave up his position as the curator of the London Kingston Museum and accepted a similar position in the United States, which he hated, and with a lower salary - coincidentally, this allowed him to conveniently contact the J\u00e4ger of Saxony! Finally, he secretly arrived in New York before the scheduled date.\" [2442] Reine sighed: \"Percy, you are truly brilliant.\" [2443] \"And -\" Percy continued enthusiastically, \"Hamnett being the axeman is based on the fact that only he does not know where the manuscript is hidden, so he must slash blindly, like the real act of the one with the axe... It is easy to recreate the scene with two Sedgwick in the house. Hamnett was slashing blindly in William's bedroom upstairs when William came in, took the things from the hidden place in the study. They met soon after, and Hamnett saw the document in William's hand, so he swung the axe at his watch and wrist. During the struggle, Hamnett's single lens fell and broke. Hamnett shot William, dragged the body into -\"\n[2444] \"No!\" Rowe shouted, \"Percy, shut up. Mr. Reine, listen to me. Everything I just said can be agreed upon to some extent - William and Hamnett are the two people in the house, William is the one who took the document, and Hamnett is the masked man and the axeman. But it was not William who was killed by Hamnett, but Hamnett who was killed by William! The corpse in the rubble could be either of them. I believe that the person who called himself Hamnett and was almost starving when we found him in the house is actually William!\" [2445] \"Gordon,\" Percy countered, \"that - that is stupid. You forgot that the original key to the house was found on the corpse, which proves that the corpse is William's.\" [2446] \"Ah, no, Perkins,\" Lane interjected, \"that doesn't make sense logically. Gordon, go on. Why do you think this clever argument is wrong?\" [2447] \"Based on psychology, sir. I admit it's hard to find evidence to support my point of view. I believe the person lying in the hospital bed lied about his identity because, as William Sedley, he was a wanted fugitive by the French police; as a survivor, he now has the documents and wants to dispose of them freely. Don't forget that he knows all about it and can say things that are in his favor. The inspector and the reporter talked the night before, and all the details were published in the newspaper, and the rest he could find out the next morning from the reporter.\" [2448] Lane chuckled oddly. \"Gordon, I agree theoretically that this motive could be established, and this argument is very clever. But who placed the bomb?\" [2449] Perkins and Rowe exchanged glances. Then both hastily agreed that the bomb was placed by a third party twenty-four hours before the murder, with the sole purpose of destroying the documents, for reasons unknown. After placing the bomb, the third party thought his work was done and disappeared from the scene. [2450] The elderly gentleman grumbled. \"But what about the kidnapping? Why did the survivor \u2013 whether it was William or Hamnet \u2013 deliberately get involved in this mess and then be helplessly discovered by the police? Do you remember how exhausted and hungry he was when we found him?\" [2451] \"That's easy,\" Percy retorted, \"whether it's William or Hamnett, their motives are the same: to frame the deceased for the fake kidnapping, thus strengthening the apparent innocence of the conspirator.\" [2452] Rowe nodded, somewhat doubtfully. [2453] \"What about Donohue?\" asked the inspector. [2454] \"If Hamnett is the survivor,\" answered Percy, \"then he is the one who kidnapped Donohue because Donohue saw him leaving the Alley's house and thought he was a co-conspirator with William. He may have thought that by kidnapping Donohue, he could get the location of the hidden documents from him\u2014remember he threatened to torture him?\" [2455] \"But if William is the survivor\u2026\" Rowe pointed out sharply, \"then it was he who kidnapped Donohue because Donohue was following him and might have threatened his plan.\" [2456] \"So now the question is,\" Reen continued, \"you both agree that Hamnet Sedley and William Sedley are involved in this crime, but you disagree on who killed whom. I must say, that's quite a predicament!\" [2457] \"Good heavens!\" the sergeant suddenly exclaimed, his eyes bulging, \"this couldn't have come at a worse time!\" [2458] \"Dad, what do you mean?\" Petty asked. [2459] \"Percy, before you came back, Reen told us that he believes this British man may have been lying about his identity, and we have a way to test if he's telling the truth!\" [2460] \"A way to test?\" Percy furrowed his brow, \"I can't think of one.\" [2461] \"It's actually quite simple,\" Rene said as he stood up. \"We need to go to Britain. Gordon, did you leave the man who claims to be Hamnet Sedley there?\" [2462] \"Yes, sir.\" [2463] \"Excellent. Let's go. This should only take five minutes to resolve.\" [2464] Chapter 29\n[2465] Visual Illusion\n[2466] They found the person who called himself Hamnet Sedley working with Dr. Jott in the library director's office. The director was somewhat surprised when they walked in, but the British man quickly stood up and smiled over to them. His pleasant demeanor faded as he saw their serious expressions. [2467] \"It's a pleasure to see you,\" he said in a friendly tone, but the smile disappeared when he saw their stern faces. \"I hope there's no trouble.\" [2468] \"We all hope so.\" The sergeant said tersely, \"Dr. Jott, would you mind if we speak with Dr. Sedley privately? It's a matter of great importance.\" [2469] \"Importance?\" The director had risen from his seat and stood still, looking at each of them before lowering his head and rummaging through some papers. \"Oh\u2014of course.\" A blush slowly rose on his face. He moved around the desk and quickly left the room. Dr. Sedley remained still, and there was silence in the room for a moment. Then Sam nodded to Raine, who took a step forward. [2470] \"Dr. Sedley,\" Raine's face showed no emotion, \"For...let's say, for scientific interest, we must conduct a very simple test on you...Percy, lend me your briefcase for a moment.\" [2471] \"A test?\" The Englishman's face showed displeasure, and he put his hand in his coat pocket. [2472] Bishop quickly handed the handbag to Rain. He opened the handbag, looked inside, took out a brightly colored handkerchief, and then closed the handbag. \"Very well, sir, please tell me, what color is this handkerchief?\" [2473] Bishop held his breath, his eyes widening with some realization, while the others stared blankly at the scene. [2474] Dr. Sedley blushed, a complex expression surging on his hawk-like face. He took a step back and said sternly, \"This is ridiculous. What is the purpose of this childish game?\" [2475] Rain whispered, \"Of course, just to confirm the innocent color of this little handkerchief, no harm, right?\" [2476] There was a silence. Then the British man spoke without turning around, his tone stiff, \"Blue.\" - The handkerchief had green, yellow, and white colors. [2477] \"Dr. Sedra's tie, Reine?\" Reine continued to ask, his expression unchanged. [2478] The British man swayed, his eyes pained. \"Brown.\" - It was actually sapphire blue. [2479] \"Thank you.\" Reine returned the handkerchief and briefcase to Perkins. \"Inspector, this man is not Dr. Hamnet Sedra, he is William Sedra, sometimes called Dr. Alessi.\" [2480] The British man suddenly collapsed into a chair, burying his face in his hands. [2481] \"God, how did you know?\" Sam whispered. [2482] Raine sighed. \"Inspector, this is basic work. On May 6th, Dr. Alessi, or William Sedra, went to your office and entrusted you with that envelope. That person could not be Dr. Hamnett Sedra, as he himself said. Dr. Hamnett Sedra was in London on May 7th for a reception in his honor. The man with the envelope, of course, was the one who wrote the symbols inside - he admitted as much in your office that morning. What does the paper and symbols represent?\" [2483] \"Ah, just... damn, I don't know.\" The inspector said. [2484] Raine tiredly said: \"The paper was a light gray, with the heading 'Saxony Library' in a deeper gray. This and the form of the symbols immediately reminded me.\" [2485] \"What does it mean? We just made a mistake. You were lucky, you just guessed right.\" [2486] \"That's right. In other words, William Sedra wrote Wm SHe upside down on the paper. To correctly read those symbols, one must turn the paper upside down, which is very unusual. Someone picks up a paper with a heading, intending to write on it, and instinctively puts the paper right side up \u2013 that is, with the name and address at the top. However, the person who wrote these symbols took the paper and wrote it upside down! Why?\" [2487] \"I see.\" Percy sighed. \"Unless it was an accident, he couldn't see the printed markings!\" [2488] \"Yes, dear, that's exactly it. It seems impossible, but it's more likely that Dr. Aless accidentally turned the paper over and wrote a few letters, not realizing it would make any difference to the person who saw the symbols later. However, another possibility is logically possible, and I cannot ignore it. I asked myself: if it were true, what kind of miracle could this phenomenon create? Why didn't Dr. Aless see the deep gray print on the Saxon paper? Was he blind? But that's hard to believe. The person who came to your office seemed to have good eyesight. Then I remembered something else, and suddenly saw the answer... the mustache.\" [2489] The British man lifted his pain-filled eyes, now filled with curiosity. He murmured, \"Mustache?\" [2490] \"You know,\" Reine smiled, \"he still doesn't understand what's wrong with the fake mustache he's wearing! Mr. Sedra, the mustache you wore that day was terrifying! It was a shocking blue-green, and who knows what other colors!\" [2491] Sedgwick opened his mouth wide, groaning, \"Oh, I bought it at a costume shop. I guess I didn't explain it well enough. The shopkeeper thought I wanted a... a ridiculous mustache for some crazy thing like dressing up as a ghost...\u201d\n[2492] \"Unfortunately,\" Renault said coldly, \"but the mustache and the note match. I think the person who wrote the symbol is very likely to be completely colorblind. I've heard of such cases, so I asked Dr. Martini. He told me that complete colorblindness is extremely rare. But when it happens, such a person sees all colors as different shades of gray, like a pencil sketch. He also mentioned another possibility - that he may not be completely colorblind, but has no color perception. That's better than being colorblind, but he can't see the differences in brightness of printed colors. When Dr. Martini checked the Saxon library note, he was quite sure that the person who wrote the symbol was affected by this visual defect.\" [2493] The British man moved, growled, \"I've never seen a color.\" [2494] They were silent for a moment. Renault sighed and continued, \"So I believe in my heart that Dr. Alessi is colorblind. Sir, you were just tormented by the same situation. You guessed the colors of Miss Sam's handkerchief and Mr. Rowe's tie, not knowing the real colors. Now you call yourself Hamnett Sedgwick, but Hamnett Sedgwick is not colorblind! We first saw him in this museum's Saxon room. He checked the repaired cabinet - the one with the stolen 1599 Jagger - and correctly identified the colors of the book covers in it, as well as the different shades of the same color. He could distinguish that a book cover was golden brown, which a colorblind person could not distinguish such subtle differences. Since you are either William or Hamnett, and Hamnett's vision is normal, while William is colorblind - you are colorblind, obviously you are William, and this reasoning is very simple. I proposed this test to see if you were lying. You did lie. Most of the story you told us in the hospital was fabricated, although I suspect there were some truths. Now please tell the whole story to my friend.\" [2495] He sat down in a chair and wiped his lips again. [2496] \"Okay,\" the British man's voice was low, \"I am William Zedra.\" [2497] When he first visited the inspector, he was Dr. Aless. He gave the letter with the symbol to the inspector; in case something happened to him while he was searching for Shakespeare's documents, it could serve as a clue - at the time, he thought the likelihood of something happening was very small. On June 20th, he didn't call because he couldn't, the very small possibility had occurred. His brother Hamnett - William found out then - accepted the position of director of the British Museum, with the sole purpose of approaching the 1599 Jagger in the Saxon collection. William stole the Jagger from the museum the night he did, and then Hamnett kidnapped William, which happened shortly after Donohue appeared, on the same night Donohue was also kidnapped by Hamnett. The Irishman had been unconscious for a while, so his sense of time was distorted... from William stealing the Jagger to the police rescuing him from the abandoned house, he was a prisoner. [2498] No matter how Hamnett threatened him, William refused to reveal the location of the documents. Of course, Donohue didn't know anything, and couldn't tell Hamnett anything. Hamnett, in order to go to the museum to work and pretend to be innocent, came and went to the place of confinement in a hurry, and couldn't do it regularly, so he became desperate. One day, he told William that he knew the documents were in William's house, and he put a bomb in the cellar to blow up the house and the documents! The bomb was made by a black market chemist on his own. It was then that William realized that his brother's true intention was not to preserve the Shakespeare documents, but to destroy them! [2499] \"But why?\" Rowe raised his fist and roared, \"That's... that's the most barbaric act of destruction! Oh, why destroy it?\" [2500] \"Is your brother insane?\" Percy exclaimed. [2501] The British man clenched his lips, looking at Reine, who was calmly looking into the distance. \"I don't know,\" he said. [2502] Hamnet set the timer on the bomb for twenty-four hours later. William realized that if he let the bomb go off, the documents would be destroyed and forever lost. He had to give up resistance, knowing that any delay would be more advantageous. He might be able to untie himself and save the documents. So he told Hamnet the location and method of opening the secret cabinet, but he couldn't escape. Hamnet proudly told him that he would go back to William's house and destroy the documents himself, and that he had plenty of time. He would remove the bomb's safety pin\u2026 Hamnet took William's key\u2014the original one\u2014and William never saw him alive again.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWe're dealing with an honest, even conscientious thief - something that has never been seen in the history of theft. The true Villon (1)! And more?\" [955] \"I think things are clear,\" the young man suddenly said. \"He returned the original fake Jagger without cutting open the cover, which means he is very familiar with rare books. I can tell you, I've seen the fake book, and it's not bad at all, something that most people wouldn't notice. He checked the book and immediately found that it was not real. Since he was only looking for the genuine 1599 Jagger, he didn't even touch the fake one and returned it.\" [956] \"So, he's probably a collector or something like that, right?\" Percy whispered. [957] \"Yes, dear, that's a very good analysis,\" the old man said, getting up and pacing around the room. \"We've put together a very enlightening picture. A scholar, an antiquarian, a collector, fundamentally honest, who went to great lengths to commit theft in order to obtain\u2014I'm sure\u2014a paper hidden inside an old and valuable book. Interesting, isn't it?\" [958] Sam interjected, \"I wonder what he could gain from it?\" [959] \"The opening, or the recess,\" Roe thought for a moment, \"is about five inches long and three inches wide. If it's a piece of paper, it might be folded and could be very old.\" [960] \"I think so,\" Raine murmured. \"Although perhaps not. Well, things are quite clear now. I think I should do some small investigations of my own.\" [961] \"About Donoho?\" The sergeant asked hopefully. [962] Lain smiled. \"No. That's your job; you'll do it better than I would. I'm thinking of...,\" he continued, frowning, \"a little research. You know, I have a rather remarkable library of my own.\" [963] \"It's a scholar's paradise,\" Roe said enviously. [964] \"What kind of research?\" Pechinsk asked. [965] \"Dear, check if the damaged Jagger's leather binding is the original. If that doesn't help, it can at least clarify some things. The leather binding of this book could provide clues to determine the year the hidden item was placed