What's all this about Gordon, Petty?\" the sergeant growled. \"So familiar.\" [982] \"All right, all right, sergeant,\" J. R. Rine interceded. \"Don't let your personal feelings get in the way of the discussion. You know you're an old fossil. Young people aren't like they used to be.\" [983] \"Dad,\" Peasmarsh blushed. [984] \"Not like your time,\" Roarke added. [985] \"Introductions, flirting, kissing in dark corners,\" Rine continued with a smile. \"Well, sergeant, you have to accept these things. I said Clurman was a slippery character, trying to cover his tracks, but there are some strange things going on that need looking into.\" [986] \"Well, anyway,\" the sergeant grumbled, \"I still don't like... huh? I didn't think of that. If so, I think we should ask Klabber a few questions.\" [987] Pencils examined the cigarette butt, his voice low: \"Dad, you know, this makes me think. Why don't we investigate Dr. Sedlar first instead of bothering Mr. Klabber?\" [988] \"Patti, you mean Britain?\" [989] \"Let's not be greedy. How about the shipping company?\" [990] \"Shipping company? What's that supposed to mean?\" [991] \"Just to see if there's any drama,\" Peachins murmured. [992] Forty-five minutes later, the sergeant put down the phone, wiping his brow with a trembling handkerchief. He finally sighed: \"Well, the show's about to start. It's too absurd... You know what the shipping company's manager just told me?\" [993] \"Oh, Dad,\" Peachins said, \"you're teasing us. What did he say?\" [994] \"There's no Hamnet Sedra on the passenger list!\" [995] They all looked at each other in surprise. Then Gordon Rowe whistled, putting out his cigarette in the sergeant's ashtray. \"So that's it,\" he murmured. \"The famous Dr. Sedra...\"\n[996] \"Good, I like it,\" Percy murmured. \"I like it a lot.\" [997] \"Good heavens, he's a fake!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Listen, kid, you can't tell a soul. Not a word, or else I'll\u2014\"\n[998] \"Alright, alright, sergeant,\" Reine interrupted him gently, leaning back on the leather sofa with many wrinkles on his forehead, \"Don't be in such a hurry. A good set-up doesn't necessarily make a good play, and a suspicious situation can't convict someone. Why did you describe Sedra to the secretary?\" [999] \"Well,\" Sam snorted, \"he checked the passenger list and couldn't find the guy's name. I described Sedra to the secretary and asked him to check with the ship's staff. The ship docked this morning, and they're all still on duty, so he did it right away. Well, Sedra not only isn't on the passenger list, but no one remembers anyone on board who looks like Sedra!\" [1000] \"Interesting,\" Rowe pondered. [1001] \"I admit the strange smell has grown stronger,\" muttered the old gentleman, \"strange, very strange\u2026\"\n[1002] \"Can't you see what this means?\" Percy exclaimed, \"It means that Sedra has been in the country at least four days!\" [1003] \"How did you figure that out, Percy?\" his father asked. [1004] \"He didn't fly across the Atlantic, did he? You remember I called the shipping company last Thursday to ask when the next ship from Britain was due in port - Sally Bostwick wrote to me saying she was coming, but she didn't say when. They said there was a ship on Saturday, the next one was today, and there were no other ships due in port. Since today is Tuesday, I figured this British fellow must have been in New York at least four days - at least since last Saturday.\" [1005] \"Even longer,\" Rowe frowned, \"Sedra! Unbelievable!\" [1006] \"You might check the Saturday ship,\" Raine said casually. [1007] The sergeant reached for the phone, then sat back down. \"I have a better idea, kill two birds with one stone,\" he pressed a button, and Miss Brody appeared in the office as if by magic, \"Did you bring your notebook? Good, send a telegram to Scotland Yard!\" [1008] \"To \u2013 to whom, Sergeant?\" Miss Brody stammered, surprised to see the tall, strong young man at the door. [1009] \"Scotland Yard. I want this slippery fellow to see how we do things here!\" The sergeant's face turned red, \"Don't you know where Scotland Yard is? London, England!\" [1010] \"Yes \u2013 yes, sir,\" Miss Brody hastily replied. [1011] \"Mark it for Inspector Trench, T-r-e-n-c-h. 'Request all information on the former director of the London Golden Museum, Hamnet Sedlar, now in New York. Include departure date from Britain, physical description, social connections, reputation, and any other available information. Keep confidential. Best regards. Send immediately.'\" [1012] Miss Brawdy stumbled towards Rowley. [1013] \"Wait, how did you spell Sedlar's name?\" [1014] \"S-e-d-d-l-e-r,\" Miss Brawdy stammered, her face pale with excitement. [1015] The inspector took a deep breath and smiled. \"Good, good, Miss Brawdy,\" he said calmly, \"Don't faint. It's all right. Good heavens, can't you even spell? It's S-e-d-l-a-r!\" [1016] \"Oh, yes, sir.\" Miss Broude quickly ran away. [1017] \"Poor Miss Broude,\" Pecchini chuckled. \"Dad, you always scare her like that. Maybe it's because of the strange young man around... Oh, Mr. Lane, what's wrong?\" She spoke with caution. [1018] Lane looked surprised and stared at Sam as if he had never seen him before, or as if he wasn't looking at him, and then jumped up. [1019] \"Good heavens!\" he exclaimed. \"So that's it!\" He paced around the room, muttering to himself. [1020] \"What's going on?\" the inspector asked, surprised. [1021] \"This name! This name! Hamnet Sedley... Good heavens, it's... it's... unbelievable! If it's a coincidence, then there's no justice in the world.\" [1022] \"Name?\" Percy frowned. \"What's so strange about a name, Mr. Lane? It sounds like proper English to me.\" [1023] Gordon Rowe's mouth hung open, like a digger, and the mischievous glint in his brown eyes was replaced by a look of astonishment and comprehension. [1024] Mr. Lane stopped pacing, rubbing his chin, and burst out in a series of low chuckles. \"Of course, it's proper English, Percy. You have a knack for getting to the heart of the matter. You're absolutely right. Not only English, but historical English. Ha! Gordon, I see your eyes shining. He stopped laughing, suddenly sitting down, and spoke in a serious tone, \"I knew I had heard that name before.\" [1025] The inspector looked blank. \"I haven't heard of any trouble.\" [1026] \"Well, Percy, you're more educated than your father. You've studied British literature, haven't you?\" [1027] \"Of course.\" [1028] \"Have you studied Elizabethan literature?\" [1029] Percy's cheeks flushed. \"That\u2014that was a long time ago.\" [1030] The old gentleman shook his head dejectedly. \"Typical modern education. So you've never heard of Hamnet Sedley. Gordon, tell them who Hamnet Sedley is!\" [1031] \"Hamnet Sedley\u2014\" Mr. Rowe's voice was a bit stunned, \"one of William Shakespeare's closest friends.\" [1032] \"Shakespeare!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Rayne, are you mistaken? Are you all crazy? What does old Shakespeare have to do with this?\" [1033] \"It has a lot to do with it. I began to think\u2026\" Rayne whispered, \"Right, Gordon, yes. It's just\u2026\" He pondered, shaking his head, \"You must know. Sedley\u2026 Oh my!\" [1034] \"I'm still not quite getting it,\" Percy complained. \"In that regard, I agree with my father. Of course\u2026\"\n[1035] \"This Sedley guy must be a wandering Jew, right?\" the sergeant joked. \"He can't be over three hundred years old, can he?\" He laughed merrily. [1036] \"Ha, ha!\" Mr. Rawlins sighed deeply. [1037] \"I don't mean our friend is Ahasuerus (2),\" Mr. Raine smiled, \"So far, nothing has been so exaggerated. But what I mean is, Dr. Hamnet Sadler - former director of the Kingston Museum in London, future director of the British Museum in New York, English, cultured, a book collector... Oh, Dr. Sadler is a direct descendant of Shakespeare's friend, it's not completely impossible.\" [1038] \"The family from Stratford?\" Percyns pondered. [1039] The old man shrugged, \"We know nothing about them.\" [1040] \"I think -\" Rawlins said slowly, \"The Sadler family comes from Gloucestershire.\" [1041] \"What difference does that make?\" Pecham protested. \"If Dr. Sedgwick is the descendant of Shakespeare's close friend, does the Sedgwick family have anything to do with this copy of the Jagger edition of 'The Passionate Pilgrim' from 1599, which caused all this commotion?\" [1042] Mr. Reen said quietly: \"Dear, the problem is here. Inspector, you sent a telegram to your British friends at Scotland Yard, and it gave us such inspiration. Maybe we can find out... who knows? 'The Passionate Pilgrim' itself is impossible... but that... \" He fell silent again. [1043] The inspector sat helplessly, looking at his friends and then at his daughter. Young Gordon Rowe stared at Reen, and Pecham stared at Rowe. [1044] Reen suddenly stood up and reached for his cane. They watched him silently. [1045] \"Strange,\" he said. \"Very strange.\" He nodded, lost in thought, and left the inspector's office. [1046] --- --- --- --- ---\n[1047] (1) Fran\u00e7ois Villon (1431-1463), a French poet known for his lyrical works, was often involved in drunken brawls, fights, and theft, committing numerous crimes. [1048] (2) Ahasuerus, a Persian king mentioned in the Bible. [1049] Chapter 11\n[1050] 3HS wM\n[1051]Dromio cheerfully muttered curses at the traffic policeman as he drove the black Lincoln away from Fifth Avenue. He skillfully maneuvered the car through the maze of vehicles, pulling up to a red light on Sixth Avenue. [1052]Mr. Jury sat silently in the back seat, tapping the sharp edge of a yellow sheet of paper against his lips. He had read the words written on the paper over a dozen times, his brow furrowed. It was a telegram, dated \"June 21 - 12:06 AM.\" The telegram had been delivered to Hamlet House in Westchester at dawn. [1053]\"It's strange for Sam to wire me at this hour,\" the old man thought to himself. \"In the middle of the night! It couldn't be... an emergency? Not likely...\"\n[1054]Dromio leaned over to honk the horn. A car and another were locked in a tangle at the corner, refusing to yield, with a line of cars behind them, the situation truly dismal. Jury looked ahead and saw the chaos spread to Fifth Avenue, so he leaned forward and tapped Dromio's ear. [1055]\"I think I'll walk the rest of the way,\" he said. \"Just one block. Meet me at Sam's office.\" [1056] He got out of the car, still holding the telegram, and carefully placed it in his coat pocket before walking towards Broadway. [1057] He found Sam Detective Agency in disarray. Miss Broadly sat in the reception area, her tense expression mirroring the chaos around her, her eyes sorrowfully fixed on Percy. Percy paced behind the railing, looking like an enraged sergeant, biting his lip and urgently glancing at the clock on the wall. [1058] At the sound of the door opening, she jumped up, Miss Broadly letting out a soft shriek. [1059] \"You've finally arrived!\" Percy exclaimed, gripping the old gentleman's arm tightly. \"I thought you'd never come. You're a lifesaver!\" [1060] Lean was startled as Percy embraced him, his arms wrapping around his neck, and passionately kissed his cheek. \"Dear child,\" Lean protested, \"you're trembling! What's happened? The telegram from the commissioner was so cryptic, it didn't tell me anything. Is he alright?\" [1061] \"He's doing wonderfully,\" Percy sneered, her eyes brightening as she touched the earrings on her ear, \"Now, let's get to the body!\" She pushed open the door to the chief's office, revealing an elderly, haggard gentleman with red-rimmed eyes and a pale complexion, stiffly sitting in a swivel chair, staring determinedly at the items on the desk. [1062] \"Hooray!\" he exclaimed, staggering to his feet, \"Good Lord! Pet, I told you you could count on the old coot! Sit down, Mr. Reen, sit down. It's so good to see you.\" [1063] Mr. Reen sank into the leather sofa. \"Goodness, what a welcome! I feel like a prodigal son. All right, tell me what's going on. I'm about to burst with curiosity.\" [1064] Sam picked up his meticulously studied item. \"See this?\" [1065] \"Well, my eyesight is perfectly fine, so I saw it.\" [1066] The inspector grinned. \"Alright, we're going to open it.\" [1067] Rayne looked from Sam to Peachins. \"But \u2014 alright, do as you please. Inspector, is that why you called me here?\" [1068] \"We sent you a telegram,\" Peachins blurted out.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWhen did this person come? Which day?\" [1283] \"Let me think. Four-five\u2014about seven weeks ago. Oh, I remember now. It was early in the morning, on that day\u2014May 6th.\" [1284] \"May 6th!\" Pechins shouted. \"Dad, Reen sir, did you hear that?\" [1285] \"I heard it, Pett,\" said Mr. Rowe displeasedly. \"You're making it sound like March 15th (1). Strange!\" [1286] Crabbe's bright little eyes darted from one person to another, and they all seemed to be hiding a malicious glee, as if he were being played for a fool. Mr. Rains murmured, \"This tall, thin British gentleman came on May sixth and pulled a little trick to get your letters and envelopes. Very well, Mr. Crabbe, we're making progress. Once we settle this, we won't bother you anymore. Did he tell you his name?\" [1287] Crabbe smirked and looked impatiently at him. \"Did he tell me his name, Mr. Rains? You're so busy asking such a question. Did he tell me his name? Of course, he told me his name. Hmm, I can't remember. Oh, I've got it!\" He handed Mr. Rains a small business card, and Peachum quickly stood up, and all four of them looked at the name on the card. [1288] The card was very cheap, and in bold capital letters, the surname was printed:\n[1289] There was nothing else on the card\u2014no address, no phone number, no first name. [1290] \"Dr. Aless!\" exclaimed Peachum, eyebrows furrowed. [1291]\"Dr. Aless!\" The detective mumbled. [1292]\"Dr. Aless!\" Rowe pondered. [1293]\"Dr. Aless!\" Klaber nodded with a sideways glance. [1294]\"Dr. Aless!\" The old gentleman spoke with a peculiar tone, and everyone turned to look at him, but he kept staring at the business card. \"Good heavens, it seems impossible. Dr. Aless... Perkins, detective, Gordon,\" he suddenly asked, \"Do you know who Dr. Aless is?\" [1295]\"Not at all familiar.\" Perkins looked at him with sharp eyes. [1296] \"Never heard of him.\" The sergeant said. [1297] \"A little familiar.\" Rowe said as he thought. [1298] \"Oh, Gordon, I thought it would jolt your memories of your student days. He\u2014\"\n[1299] Klopeck made a grotesque dance movement, like a trained monkey. His gold-rimmed glasses slipped down his nose, and his smile was terrifying. \"I can tell you who Dr. Aless is.\" He said, puckering his withered lips like a playboy. [1300] \"Oh, you know?\" Reen quickly said. [1301] \"I mean, I can tell you who he really is, where he is. Everything! \" Klopp chuckled dryly. \"It's all coming back to me now.\" [1302] \"Oh, for heaven's sake,\" the sergeant gruffly said. \"Who is he?\" [1303] \"The moment I saw him at the museum, I knew who he was. Hmm, right.\" The elderly librarian cleared his throat. \"You didn't see him avoid my gaze, did you? He knew I recognized him, that rare scoundrel! I'll tell you, the man who came to see me seven weeks ago and left this calling card, claiming to be Dr. Ales, is\u2014Herman Sedlar!\" [1304] \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[1305] (1) The day Julius Caesar was killed. [1306] Chapter 14\n[1307] The Battle of the Bookworms\n[1308] In a private dining room of a downtown restaurant, the people at the table were all sorting out their jumbled thoughts. Krapf revealed the secret with a mocking, triumphant air, leaving everyone stunned for a moment. Hamnet Sedley was the mysterious Dr. Aless! Krapf gloated, licking his lips and sending them to the door. Their last glimpse of him saw him standing in the doorway of the Saxon mansion, framed by the Ionic columns, his hands rubbing together as if the legs of a cricket. He hunched his shoulders and tilted his head, as if saying, \"Well, you think your Dr. Sedley is your Dr. Aless, don't you? Old Krapf isn't a fool, is he? He exuded a sense of victory, as if he had satisfied his violent urges on a mob after executing a private lynching. [1309] Gordon Rowe had been preoccupied the whole time and now joined the small group, sitting quietly and watching the sunlight filter through the car window onto Percy's hair, but he seemed not to really see. [1310] \"There's one very odd thing,\" Mr. Philo Vance said as they sat down around the table, \"I admit I don't understand. That wretched old man, he was certainly impressive\u2014full of dramatic elements\u2014and what he said was all true. He's the kind of man who likes to tell the truth, especially when he knows it will hurt. But... Hamnet Sedley! Of course not.\" [1311] \"If Claver says his guest was Sedley,\" the young Rowe said dejectedly, \"then you can bet your boots it was Sedley.\" [1312] \"No, Gordon,\" Percy sighed. \"Sedley couldn't have been the one who visited Claver on May 6th. The board of the London Guildhall Museum held a farewell dinner for Dr. Ales on May 7th, so it couldn't have been him. Dr. Ales visited Claver in New York on May 6th. He's not a ghost; he couldn't have crossed the Atlantic in one night.\" [1313] \"Well, it's just too eerie. I know Claver, and I'll tell you, he's not lying. Every time he tells the truth and causes a stir, he's delighted, just like Mr. Vance said.\" [1314] \"Claver was absolutely certain,\" Percy said despairingly, poking at his roast. \"He swore the man was Sedley.\" [1315] \"What's the big deal?\" Inspector Belsey glared at Rowe. \"The old devil is lying, and that's that.\" [1316] \"Well,\" Raine said, \"he could be making up a story. These old bookworms can be jealous of each other's careers. Anyway, we're not getting anywhere with this. The whole thing is too bizarre...there's something I need to tell you about Dr. Allis.\" [1317] \"Oh, yes! You were about to tell us, but Klabber interrupted...isn't that name fictitious?\" [1318] \"Of course not! That's why it's so unusual, dear. Gordon, when you were at the Saxon's, you seemed to be on the verge of remembering something. Do you remember who Dr. Allis is now?\" [1319] \"I'm sorry, sir, I thought I remembered. Probably I came across the name while doing research.\" [1320] \"It's possible. I've never met Dr. Allis myself, and I know nothing about him personally, but I do know one thing. Unless this isn't a remarkable coincidence, such a person does exist, and he's a very clever and well-educated scholar of literature.\" The old gentleman thoughtfully chewed a stalk of celery, \"A few years ago\u2014oh, eight or ten years ago, there was an article in the Sturford Quarterly\u2014a journal devoted to bibliography\u2014\"\n[1321] \"Right!\" Rowe exclaimed, \"I used to receive it regularly when I was at university.\" [1322] \"That reminds you. The point is, the author of that article was Dr. Allais.\" [1323] \"A British magazine?\" Sam asked. [1324] \"Yes. I don't remember the specifics, but Dr. Allais discussed some ridiculous and lengthy controversies about Bacon, and some of the things he said greatly offended me. So, I wrote a long article in my own name to refute him, which was published in the quarterly. Dr. Allais was also quite angry and responded in the magazine's correspondence column. We went back and forth for several issues in the quarterly.\" [1325] \"I remember,\" Rowe said eagerly, his chin jutting forward, \"It was a heated debate. That's the guy, no doubt about it!\" [1326] \"Do you know where he lives?\" the Chief suddenly asked. [1327] \"Unfortunately, no.\" [1328] \"We can look up this magazine.\" [1329] \"I'm afraid not, Chief. Mr. Roche can tell you that The Stoke Quarterly went out of print five years ago.\" [1330] \"Damn it! Alright, I'll send another telegram to Trunch, asking him to look into it. You want\u2014\"\n[1331] \"By the way, Gordon,\" said the old gentleman, \"have you had a chance to check on the binding of the Jaggard edition of the 1599 folio we discussed? Any leads on a secret related to the binding?\" [1332] Rowe shrugged. \"Nothing so far. I did trace the binding back to about a hundred and fifty years ago\u2014it's not something a person would do. The current binding is at least that old, and as for the documents inside\u2014nothing so far. No leads yet.\" [1333] \"Hmm,\" said Lanyon, his eyes flashing, and then he resumed eating his salad. [1334] Percyns pushed his plate away impatiently. \"Oh, I can't chew it. This damned case is getting on my nerves. The whole business of Dr. Siddar being the Ales is so absurd, and yet it keeps going around in my head, and I can't get rid of it. Other things are so clear\u2014\"\n[1335] \"For example\u2014\" said the inspector skeptically. [1336] \"Dr. Aless's clues. Dad, you know that the man with the rainbow mustache who came to our office on May 6th was Dr. Aless.\" [1337] \"How did you come to that conclusion?\" the young Rowe whispered. [1338] \"He went to visit the Saxons that morning, took the library's stationery from them. He must have put on that ridiculous outfit in some hotel's washroom. He wrote down the symbols - annoying symbols! - put on his strange outfit, and went to Dad's office. These things are clear.\" [1339] \"It seems possible,\" said the old gentleman. [1340] \"He didn't think he'd be - killed,\" Percy bit his lip, \"he thought no one would know his secret, his million-dollar secret. Doesn't that sound stupid?... But he was a cunning man, he wouldn't take any risks. If he had called on the 20th and was fine, nothing would have happened, the envelope would still be sealed; if he hadn't called, we would have opened the envelope, seen the Saxon stationery, tracked down Klaber, and discovered this strange Dr. Aless - he must have told Klaber that fabricated story, so Klaber would remember - by the time we went looking for him, we would have known his name and his profession\u2026\"\n[1341] \"What an awful logical analysis!\" Young Rowe smiled slightly. [1342] \"So he asked you not to open the envelope unless I was there,\" Rene said quietly. \"He knew I would remember our debate in the quarterly; so I was brought in to confirm that Dr. All\u00e8s was a book collector.\" [1343] \"He must have planned it all from the start. If things didn't go well, like now, we'd have to look for Dr. All\u00e8s, a bookworm or something. How do we go about it?\" [1344] \"It's easy,\" the inspector said carelessly. \"Petti, that's my job. He said if he didn't call, it meant he was in trouble, right? That means we know where he disappeared from, apart from his physical appearance, name, profession, or occupation - he must have been mixed up somewhere or killed.\" [1345] \"Well said, Inspector,\" Reth murmured. \"Right on the mark. You must collect all the police reports of murders, kidnappings, or disappearances from May 20th - the day he didn't call on time - to a few days ago.\" [1346] The sergeant growled, \"I know, I know. Do you know how much work that is?\" [1347] \"Not as bad as you think, Sergeant. What you're looking for is clear. Percy has already said it.\" [1348] \"Alright!\" Sam said glumly. \"I'll do it. But what's in it for me? I have to live, you know.\" [1349] Mr. J. B. Rathbone sent Sam the sergeant back to the office, dropped Miss Samantha Percy and Gordon Rowe off at Central Park, and signaled to Dromio, then sat back in the car, deep in thought. Now no one was watching him, he sat quietly in the back seat, clutching his cane handle tightly, staring at the back of Dromio's head, his face a picture of shifting emotions. Unlike most old men, he was not accustomed to talking to himself aloud, possibly because his deaf ear had not made him prone to such habits. He thought things out in pictures, some of which were so bizarre that he had to close his eyes to see them more clearly. [1350] The Lincoln limousine swiftly drove uptown, heading towards Westchester. [1351] After a long time, the old man opened his eyes and saw the lush trees and winding driveway leading to the garage. He leaned forward and lightly tapped Dromio's shoulder. [1352] \"Dromio, didn't I tell you to go to Dr. Martini's first?\" [1353] Dromio, the faithful driver, turned his head slightly so that his master could see his lips. \"Mr. Vance, is there an emergency? Are you feeling unwell again?\" [1354] The old gentleman smiled. \"No, I'm fine. This visit is purely out of scientific interest.\" [1355] \"Oh.\" Dromio scratched his left ear, shrugged, and pressed down on the accelerator. [1356] He parked the car in front of a house near Ephraim. The house was half-hidden in the trees, with vines and late-blooming roses growing outside. A silver-haired, imposing man sat on the porch smoking a pipe. [1357] \"Ah, Martini,\" Reine stretched his legs. \"Lucky you're home at this time.\" [1358] The imposing man widened his eyes. \"Mr. Reine, what brings you here? Please come in, please come in.\" [1359] Reine chuckled and closed the door behind him. \"Don't be surprised, old man. I'm feeling great.\" They shook hands, and Dr. Martini's tired eyes looked at him professionally. \"You look good, don't you?\" [1360] \"Excellent. How's your heart?\" [1361] \"It's beating merrily. I can't say the same for my stomach.\" [1362] They entered the doctor's house. A fluffy dog sniffed at Lane's ankle and then walked away indifferently. \"I don't understand, at my age\u2014\"\n[1363] \"Dear great man, a lifetime of theater menus doesn't do wonders for digestion. Sit down. I managed to sneak away from the hospital for a few hours. The hospital is boring, and I haven't come across an interesting case.\" [1364] Lane smiled. \"I have one for you.\" [1365] Dr. Martini took the pipe out of his mouth. \"Ah, I think I know. Not yourself, I presume?\" [1366] \"No, no.\" [1367] \"A troublesome matter?\" Martini said with a half-smile. \"I'll give up the afternoon countryside fun.\" [1368] \"No need.\" The old man leaned forward. \"This case \u2013 I believe \u2013 can be diagnosed while sitting in a chair.\" He suddenly looked around. \"Martini, I think you should close the door.\" [1369] The doctor was puzzled and then stood up, shutting the sunlight outside. [1370] \"You're mysterious,\" he said, sitting back in the chair with the pipe in his mouth. \"Confidential? I guess it's a criminal case, but there's no one here to listen \u2013\"\n[1371] Laine's gaze was stern. \"Martini, when a person goes deaf, they feel like the walls have ears. Old friend, I've gotten involved in this most incredible event, with many branches at every point\u2026\"\n[1372] Dromio, dozing at the wheel, swatted a bee that had landed on his collar, somewhat surprised. The sweet fragrance of roses intoxicated him. Martini's door had been closed for half an hour and now opened, revealing his tall figure. Dromio heard Martini say lightly, \"It's probably the only way, Mr. Laine. I must see the paper before I can give you an opinion. But even so, as I said\u2026\"\n[1373] \"You scientists!\" Dromio heard Laine's tone of impatience. \"I used to hope the problem would be clearer. But\u2026\" He shrugged and extended his hand. \"Thank you for showing interest. I think I deserve some credit. I'll bring the paper to you tonight.\" [1374] \"Ah, yes, I'll come to Hamlet's Manor tonight.\" [1375] \"Oh, nonsense! That would be too much trouble for you. I'll come here again\u2026\"\n[1376] \"Don't say that. Driving does me good, and I want to see old Quincannon. The last time I saw him, his arteries were in a bad state.\" [1377] Dromio didn't understand what was going on and opened the door. His master quickly walked down the path, stopped, and raised his eyebrows sharply, saying sharply, \"Did you see anyone lurking around here?\" [1378] Dromio was startled. \"Lurking, sir? Philo?\" [1379] \"Yes, did you see anyone else?\" [1380] Dromio scratched his ear. \"I probably dozed off for a minute or two, sir, but I don't think\u2026\"\n[1381] \"Ah, Dromio Jr.,\" the old gentleman sighed, climbing into the car, \"when will you learn to be more alert... I suppose it doesn't matter. He waved cheerfully to Dr. Martini, \"Pull over at the gas station, Dromio Jr., to the telegraph office.\" [1382] The car drove away. When they reached the gas station, Dromio Jr. found the telegraph office, and Philo Roen walked in. He glanced at the clock on the wall, then sat down at a small table, reaching for a stack of yellow paper and a chained pencil. For a few seconds, he stared at the pencil tip. The pencil was sharp, but he didn't notice. His eyes were focused on something else.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"Because there's a lunatic who insisted you be present when we opened the letter. Dad, open it! If you don't, I'll go mad.\" [1069] This was the long brown paper envelope that the eccentric gentleman with the rainbow moustache had deposited in the inspector's safe about seven weeks ago. [1070] Rayne took the envelope from Sam, feeling its weight. As he felt the rectangular envelope inside, his eyes narrowed. \"This mysterious action needs an explanation. I'd like to know more... no, no, dear, I've told you before to cultivate \u2014 ha, ha \u2014 the virtue of patience. Inspector, please proceed!\" [1071] Sam briefly recounted the story of the disguised British visitor on May 6th. Pencin added details as he went along, making the story quite complete and particularly detailed in describing the visitor. After the sergeant finished, Rine looked thoughtfully at the envelope. \"But why didn't you tell me about this beforehand? Sergeant, that's not like you.\" [1072] \"I didn't think it was necessary. Well, let's get on with it!\" [1073] \"Wait a minute, your mysterious customer didn't call you yesterday as he was supposed to?\" [1074] \"But he called on May 20th,\" the sergeant said, exasperated. [1075] \"We've been sitting here all day,\" Pencin grumbled. \"Until midnight yesterday. No word from him. Now\u2014\"\n[1076] \"Do you have a record of your conversation with this person?\" Lanyon said mildly. \"I know you have a recorder here.\" [1077] Sam pressed a button. \"Miss Broadly, bring in the conversation record for the envelope case.\" [1078] They sat there anxiously, waiting for the old man to meticulously read their conversation record with the visitor. [1079] \"Hmm.\" He put down the report. \"Very strange. Yes, the guy was deliberately disguised. Clumsy, very clumsy! Clearly unrealistic. That beard\u2026\" He shook his head. \"Alright, Inspector, I think we can start now. This is your honor.\" [1080] He stood up, threw the envelope on Sam's desk, sat down in the chair next to the desk, leaned forward, and looked focused. Percy quickly circled the desk and stood behind her father's chair. Her breathing was rapid, and her usually calm face was pale and agitated. Sam used trembling fingers to pull out the pad near Lanyon and put the envelope on it. He was sweating profusely, then looked up at Lanyon\u2014they were sitting across the table from each other\u2014and smiled helplessly. [1081] \"Okay, let's start,\" he said jokingly. \"I hope nothing jumps out and says 'April Fool's Day' or something like that.\" [1082] Percy, standing behind him, sighed in frustration because she couldn't breathe due to her nervousness. [1083] The sergeant held the letter opener, hesitated for a moment, then inserted the knife into the seal of the brown paper envelope. He deftly cut the seal, put down the knife, and peered inside the envelope. [1084] \"What is it?\" Percy exclaimed. [1085] \"Percy, you're right,\" he muttered. \"It's another envelope. \" He took out a small square envelope - light gray, also sealed, with a blank front. [1086] \"What's on the envelope?\" The old gentleman asked hastily. [1087] The inspector flipped the envelope over, his face turning ashen. [1088] Percy, standing behind him, was gasping for breath as he observed the label on the envelope. [1089] Sam licked his lips. \"It says... His voice was hoarse, \"It says... Oh my... It says: Saxon Library!\" [1090] This was the first sign - indicating that the mysterious guest with the rainbow beard might be connected to the bizarre events at the British Museum. [1091] \"The Saxon Library,\" said Reine, \"How odd.\" [1092] \"Well, I'll be damned,\" Sam exclaimed, \"What have we stumbled upon?\" [1093] The old man spoke laboriously, \"Obviously a coincidence, Inspector. Such things happen, but this coincidence is enough to make one believe...,\" his voice trailed off. He did not move his gaze from the inspector's lips, but it seemed his eyes did not see anything, for there was a glow over them, like a veil had fallen\u2014a veil that hid the sudden insight that had entered his eyes. [1094] \"But I don't understand...,\" Percy said, bewildered. [1095] Reine shuddered, the veil disappeared. \"Inspector, open it!\" he said, leaning forward, his hands resting on his chin. [1096] Sam picked up the letter opener again and inserted the blade into the seal, slowly applying pressure. The paper was stiff and reluctant to yield. [1097] Raine and Percy did not blink an eye. [1098] Sam's thick fingers reached into the envelope and pulled out a sheet of pale gray paper, the same tone as the envelope, neatly folded. He unfolded the paper. There was some text printed at one end. The inspector turned the paper over, and the text on the other side was simple: Saxon Library. The text was gray, a darker shade than the paper. He laid the paper flat on the pad, placing it between himself and Raine. They both stared at the paper, and the office fell into silence. [1099] The reason was: if the imposter was a mysterious figure, the secret he held in the inspector's hand was even more mysterious - not only mysterious, but also eerie. There was no logic in this at all. [1100] The letter had \"Saxon Library\" printed at the top, and the rest of the page was as smooth as freshly printed paper, with only a few words, or symbols, written near the center, just below the printed heading. [1101] Nothing more. No decipherable information, no signature, no mark made by pen or pencil. [1102] The inspector's old body suddenly trembled with excitement, hunched over in his chair, eyes fixed on the characters on the paper. The paper slipped from his fingers. Percy remained motionless. For a long time, no one moved. Eventually, the old man slowly shifted his gaze from the paper to Sam. In the depths of his clear eyes, there was a hint of triumph, almost a gleam of euphoria. [1103] However, the inspector muttered, \"3HS wM.\" His tone was bewildered, the syllables rolling off his tongue, as if he were trying to unravel the meaning hidden within the sounds. [1104] Ren's face showed a touch of confusion, glancing quickly at Percy. [1105] She murmured, \"3HS wM,\" as if a child repeating foreign language. [1106] The old man buried his face in his hands and sat there motionless. [1107] * * *\n[1108] \"Well!\" The inspector finally sighed deeply, \"I give up, damn it, I give up. A guy walks in dressed like a clown in a parade and leaves a bunch of crazy talk about 'a million-dollar secret' - tell you, I give up. It's a joke, someone's joke.\" [1109] Pechin swiftly circled around her father's chair and picked up the paper. She furrowed her brow