tedly, \"the small w and the capital M... Oh, God, it's confusing. I wish Gordon were here; he knows a lot of useless things.\" [1121] The sergeant slowly turned around. \"Of course, no headway.\u201d His tone was odd, \"For us, that is, Petty; for your lively Mr. Rowe, too. But don't forget, this mysterious person involves Reynolds, so he may think Reynolds would know what it means\u2026 Hey, Reynolds?\u201d\n[1122] Ren remained indifferent to this obvious challenge, and then wrinkles appeared at the corners of his eyes. \"Suspect me? Maybe it is as you think, old man.\" [1123] \"Oh, so what the hell does that mean?\" the officer asked bluntly, walking over. [1124] Ren waved his fair hand and continued to stare at the paper in front of him, muttering, \"It's strange, I believe he thought you would also know what this means.\" [1125] The sergeant flushed and stood up, walking towards the door. \"Miss Brodie! Bring in the ledger.\" [1126] Miss Broadie quickly entered, holding a pencil. [1127] \"Write a letter to Dr. Leo Shilling of the Laboratory. 'Dear Doctor: Please attend to this matter immediately. Do not disclose. Does the following series of symbols have any special meaning to you?' Then write this: '3, capital H, capital S, small space, lowercase w, capital M'. Understood?\" [1128] Miss Broadie looked up, dumbfounded. \"Understood, sir.\" [1129] \"Send a copy of the same letter to Rupert Sheff, head of the decoding group at the Washington D.C. Intelligence Bureau. Hurry up.\" [1130] Miss Broadie quickly left. [1131] The inspector said confidently, \"We should get some results.\" [1132] He sat back in his chair, lit a cigar, stretched his thick legs, and puffed a cloud of smoke toward the ceiling thoughtfully. \"The first thing to investigate, in my opinion, is the text printed at the top of the note. This fellow walks in and tells us a story that no one believes, leaves a note full of nonsense. He doesn't want us to know he's connected to Saxen, so he slipped the envelope into an unmarked brown paper bag. If anything happens to him, he wants us to open the envelope. He wants us to see the words 'Saxen Library' and investigate in that direction. So far, it seems clear.\" [1133] Rine nodded. \"I agree with your assessment.\" [1134] \"What he didn't count on was George Fisher coming here to tell me about Donohue, which led us to the British Museum and the ridiculous story of the stolen book. What's the connection between him and Saxen? I have no idea. Maybe the appearance of the Saxen note was just a coincidence.\" [1135] \"It's not a coincidence, Dad,\" Perkins said wearily. \"I don't believe it. I believe the man with the fake mustache and the British oddities are connected. The symbols on the Saxen library note are the point of connection. I think\u2014\"\n[1136] \"What?\" Sam looked shrewdly at his daughter. [1137] Perkins smiled. \"It's a silly idea, but the whole thing is crazy... I was thinking, what if the guy with the fake mustache... what if he was someone pretending to be from the Saxton family!\" [1138] \"They wouldn't be that stupid,\" the sergeant said nonchalantly. \"But Perkins, I have similar thoughts. You see, Rowley... \"\n[1139] \"Nonsense!\" Perkins exclaimed sharply, and both men looked at her. \"No! It couldn't be Gordon.\" [1140] \"Why not?\" Sam asked. \"I thought he seemed desperate to join us when we left the museum that day.\" [1141] Percy coldly said: \"I assure you his desperation has nothing to do with this case. But - it could be a personal matter. Dad, I'm not an old hag yet!\" [1142] \"I don't believe it's a personal matter,\" Sam retorted. [1143] \"Dad! Sometimes you make me want to cry. What do you dislike about Gordon? He's a good young man, as honest as... as a child. And his wrists are thick, the person who came on May 6th wasn't like that.\" [1144] \"Hmph, he's one of those bookworms, book lovers, isn't he?\" Sam grumbled. [1145] Percy bit her lip. \"Oh - go ahead and say it!\" [1146] \"Think about it,\" the inspector continued, tapping the tip of his nose. \"It couldn't be Mrs. Saxen, although I had a crazy feeling that the person might be a woman. But Mrs. Saxen is a big, fat woman, and this guy is so thin. So maybe \u2014 tell you, I haven't ruled out Rawlins yet! Maybe it's Crabtree.\" [1147] \"That's different,\" Percy shook her head. \"He fits all the external features.\" [1148] Mr. Jury Lean had been sitting quietly on the sidelines, enjoying the banter between the father and daughter, and then held up his hand. \"I beg your pardon for interrupting this profound discussion,\" he said slowly. \"My contrary opinion may completely overturn this discussion. Your guest says \u2014 and I have no reason to doubt \u2014 that if he hadn't called on the twentieth, it would mean he encountered something unusual. If young Gordon Rawlins \u2014 too ridiculous! Inspector \u2014 or Crabtree was your guest on May sixth, why haven't they disappeared or been murdered, or for some other reason immobilized?\" [1149] \"That is a fact,\" Percy said eagerly. \"Of course! Dad, I had lunch with Gordon yesterday and spoke to him this morning, and he \u2014 he didn't mention anything about it. I believe \u2014\"\n[1150] \"Listen, Petty,\" the inspector's tone had a warning note. \"Just listen to your father once! Petty, you're interested in that boy, aren't you? Is he pursuing you? Humph, I'll break his neck!\" [1151] Percy stood up, saying angrily, \"Dad!\" [1152] \"All right, all right, Sergeant,\" the old man murmured, \"don't be so stubborn. Gordon Lowe is a fine young man, and his scholarship matches yours, which is rare!\" [1153] \"I tell you, I don't love him!\" Percy called, \"Dad, you're terrible. Can't I be good to a man?\" [1154] The sergeant looked pitiful. [1155] Mr. Jules Reine stood up. \"Stop arguing. Sergeant, you're so childish. Put this paper and envelope back in the safe. We have to go to the Saxon's house.\" [1156] Chapter 12\n[1157] Good luck to me\n[1158] Traffic was heavy, and the Lincoln crawled along Fifth Avenue, Dromio sweating with impatience. But Mr. Vance seemed unruffled; he glanced at Sam and Paisin and chuckled. \"You two are a pair of bad-tempered kids. Smile!\" [1159] \"You're a pair of bad-tempered kids,\" he continued. \"What an extraordinary case! I don't think you realize how extraordinary it is.\" [1160] \"I have a headache,\" the Chief complained. [1161]\"And you, Peachum?\" [1162]\"I think\u2014\" Peachum was still watching Dromio's red back of the head, \"\u2014that those symbols mean a lot more to you than they do to us.\" [1163]The old gentleman was slightly surprised, and he leaned forward, his eyes sharp as he studied her smooth, young face. \"Perhaps so,\" he said, \"but in the past. Inspector, any progress? There's been so much going on this morning that I haven't had a chance to ask.\" [1164]\"Quite a bit,\" the inspector said wearily, \"Mr. Broude has it all down. I know you'd want to know.\" [1165]Donohue: Still missing, no trace found. [1166] Seventeen teachers: Back in Indiana. All identities checked out without suspicion. Investigation conducted with caution. Photos, features, addresses, names \u2013 listed in order. [1167] One hundred yuan note: withdrawn from a 1959 Jagger. No further information can be obtained from the serial number on the note. [1168] Blue Hats: Still no trace. [1169] The nineteenth person on the bus: No trace found. [1170] \"Inspector, is that all?\" Lestrade said, returning the report and looking somewhat disappointed, \"I thought you had sent a telegram to Scotland Yard.\" [1171] \"Never forget, do you, you old fox?\" The inspector grinned, \"Not quite, more like an elephant, eh? Yes, Scotland Yard's Trunch gave me a reply. It was like honey, arrived late yesterday. Take a look.\" [1172] He handed Reen a stack of telegrams, which the old man eagerly snatched. They watched his face, growing more serious as he read. The telegram was addressed to the inspector and read as follows:\n[1173] The Sedras are an ancient British family, dating back to the Second Crusade. Sir Hamnet Sedra was known for being Shakespeare's friend. The current Sedra is five feet eleven inches tall, weighs one hundred fifty-four pounds, has a lean build, prominent features, blue eyes, and blond hair, with no other distinguishing features. He is fifty-one years old, and his personal life is unknown.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe has lived alone in London for at least twelve years, moving from Gloucestershire to this location, which is not far from Stratford. He is a reputable antiquities appraiser, specializing in rare books, and has served as the curator of the London Kingston Museum. Recently, he accepted an invitation from James Wythe of America to become the curator of the British Museum in New York. His colleagues were surprised, as Sedra often claimed to be anti-American. He attended a farewell banquet held by the board on May 7th. Sedra has only one brother, whose whereabouts are unknown and has not been in the UK for years. The Sedra brothers have no criminal record and have led a strict and modest academic life. Hamnet left Britain on the Westerly on May 17th, Friday, and arrived in New York on May 22nd, Tuesday. The passenger list confirms that Hamnet was indeed aboard the ship. Additional information can be provided upon request. [1174] Trunch\n[1175] \"What do you think?\" The inspector asked proudly. [1176] \"It's splendid,\" Lestrade murmured, handing the telegram back, his forehead furrowed and his eyes glazed. [1177] \"Now it's clear,\" Poirot said, \"Sedley arrived in New York a week earlier than he claimed. Seven days! What did he do in New York during that time? If he was here, why lie? I don't like that 'honest' man.\" [1178] \"I've sent word to Superintendent Gregson,\" Sam said, \"to keep it quiet and discreetly track Sedley's movements between May 22nd and May 29th. It's the same person, and he matches the description. But he must have something he doesn't want us to know, and I don't like him any more than Poirot does.\" [1179] \"So, what exactly do you suspect him of?\" Lestrade asked. [1180] The inspector shrugged. \"At least in one thing, he's innocent. He couldn't be the one who left the false mustache, spoke with a British accent, and left me a note. According to Trunch's information, Sedley didn't leave England until May 17th, while this man came to see me on May 6th. But... \" He smiled. \"It could be someone else, I'll bet it is!'\" [1181] \"Is that so?\" the old gentleman said. \"Who could it be?\" [1182] \"The guy in the blue hat, throwing around rare books and hundred-dollar bills like a madman!\" Sam exclaimed excitedly. \"That madman showed up again on May 27th, which was five days after Sedra arrived in New York.\" [1183] \"Chief Inspector, that's a weak argument,\" Rene smiled. \"Similarly, the man in the blue hat could be one of the millions of people with unaccounted for movements on May 27th.\" [1184] The chief inspector swallowed his words; his disappointed expression clearly showed he didn't like it. \"I know, but\u2026\"\n[1185] \"Oh, my God!\" Percy suddenly spoke up, jumping up and hitting his head on the ceiling. \"How could I not think of it sooner?!\" [1186] \"Think of what?\" Reen asked softly. [1187] \"Symbols, symbols! That\u2014oh, I'm blind!\" [1188] Reen calmly looked at her. \"Child, what about the symbols?\" [1189] Perkins bit her handkerchief and breathed heavily through her nose. \"It's obvious\u2014\" she put away her handkerchief, sat up straight, and her eyes sparkled. \"3HS wM, can't you see it?\" [1190] \"I still can't make sense of it,\" Sam complained. [1191] \"Oh, Dad, HS must stand for Hamnet Siddall!\" [1192] The two men exchanged glances and burst out lau