ivine ignorance.\" The young man's light brown eyes narrowed to a line, \"Tracing clues?\" [1209] \"Gordon!\" Peachins suddenly said, \"What does 3HS wM stand for?\" [1210] \"Petty, for God's sake!\" the inspector gritted his teeth, \"we don't want\u2014\"\n[1211] \"Never mind, Inspector,\" Lane said calmly. \"There's no reason to keep Gordon in the dark.\" [1212] The young man's gaze shifted from Percy to the two men. \"It's all a bit confusing,\" he said. \"What's going on?\" [1213] Percy told him. [1214] \"Saxon Library,\" he muttered. \"That's really strange - it's a mystery! I think...wait, Crabbe is coming.\" [1215] The old librarian hurried into the reception room, one hand holding up his gold-rimmed glasses and his face full of suspicion as he looked at the guests. But he immediately perked up and walked over. Percy swore he could hear the librarian's bones creaking as he walked. [1216] \"Ah, Mr. Raine,\" Crabbe said with a grin, his face as leathery as a piece of old skin, \"and Miss Sam, and Inspector. What a surprise! I thought you were going out, Roe. Or is it because the lady came - Mrs. Saxon is indisposed, stomach pains. That waistline, of course, must hurt, a real tragedy. He behaved like a child, \"What can I do for you?\" [1217] \"For one thing,\" Raine smiled, cutting off the inspector's words that were already stuck in his throat. \"We wanted to see the famous Saxon Library.\" [1218] \"So that's it,\" Crabbe said, standing still, his thin shoulder drooping, his head tilted, his eyes twinkling at the guests. \"Surely a friendly visit, huh? He chuckled, showing his decaying gums. \"There's no reason why you shouldn't go in. His tone was surprisingly friendly. \"You're the first strangers...oh, Roe? Do you think we should make an exception this time?\" [1219] \"You're really a nice guy,\" Roe smiled. [1220] \"Oh, I'm not as bad as they say. Come with me.\" [1221] He led them through several opulent French corridors to a room in the east wing of the 