get the Saxon library's stationery except you?\" [1250] \"That's right. The items are kept here. Of course, if someone really wanted them -\"\n[1251] \"Yes, yes, Mr. Klauber. We understand. Gordon, please smile. I guess these rooms have been off-limits for years. Now\u2026\"\n[1252] \"So, what about the servants?\" Percy suddenly asked, ignoring the pain in Rowe's eyes. [1253] \"No, Miss Sam. It's always been a strict rule. I clean the rooms myself, Mr. Saxton insisted.\" [1254] \"When you were packing the books for Britain,\" Mr. Lane asked, \"Mr. Klauber, were you there?\" [1255] \"Of course.\" [1256] \"I was too,\" Rowe mumbled dejectedly. [1257] \"Hour by hour?\" [1258] \"Oh, certainly,\" Crabtree said. \"Mr. Rowe is in charge of the truck drivers, but I assure you, my eyes have never been closed.\" [1259] \"Excellent!\" Mr. Lane smiled. \"Inspector, it seems impossible to remove a piece of paper from here. Doesn't it seem strange to you?\" [1260] \"What do you think?\" Sam sneered coldly. [1261] Mr. Lane looked straight into the eyes of the elderly librarian and spoke quietly, \"Mr. Clapber, there's nothing mysterious about this. We have a letter from the Saxon Library \u2013 an envelope \u2013 we have to trace its origins. Your story makes things complicated.\" He suddenly had an idea and slapped his forehead. \"How could I be so stupid! Of course!\" [1262] \"A letter from me?\" Clapber was bewildered. [1263] The elderly gentleman patted Clapber on the shoulder. \"Do you often have visitors?\" [1264] \"Visitors? To the Saxon Library? Huh, tell him, Rowe.\" [1265] \"He's a bookworm par excellence,\" Rowe shrugged. \"The most loyal watchdog in the world.\" [1266] \"Well, well, you must have had visitors! Think carefully. Have there been any guests in the past few months that you can remember?\" [1267] Klabber's eyes blinked, his bony chin dropped slightly, and he ignored the questioner. Then he burst out laughing and slapped his thigh. \"Ha! Ha! I remember now.\" [1268] Raine said: \"It seems we've hit the jackpot. So, what about it, sir?\" [1269] Klabber stopped laughing as abruptly as he had started, and his bony hands clenched together. \"That's it, huh? Great. Another amazing thing... There was a man, yes, that's right! A very interesting gentleman. He came to see me several times before I agreed to see him. And then he asked - very cleverly; he, he! - to allow him to see the famous Saxon collection.\" [1270] \"Really?\" Raine exclaimed. [1271] \"He said he was a bookworm, heard many\u2014you know. So I let him in once\u2014he seemed harmless enough\u2014and let him into this room. He said he was researching something, eager to consult a book. Just a few minutes, he said\u2026\"\n[1272] \"Which book?\" Roeve furrowed his brow. \"Klaber, you never told me about this!\" [1273] \"Child, did I not? I must have forgotten.\u201d Klaber chuckled. \"It was the 1599 edition of 'The Passionate Pilgrim' by Jaggard!\" [1274] They fell silent for a moment, none daring to look at the others. [1275] \"Go on,\" Lane whispered, urging him. \"So you just handed him the book?\" [1276] Clarber smiled ugly. \"Clarber would never! I said, sir, no. I said the rules didn't allow it. He nodded, not seeming surprised, and looked around again. I began to doubt, but he went on about the book... Finally, he walked back to the desk, where there were some papers - note cards and envelopes. His eyes flickered with a strange expression, and then he said, 'Is this the stationery of the Saxon Library, Mr. Clarber?' I said, 'Yes.' He looked pitifully at me and said, 'Ha! Ha! Very interesting. It's hard to get into this place. Do you know, I made a bet with a friend that I could get in? Look, good sir, I did it!' I replied, 'Oh, you did, didn't you?' He said, 'Ha! I'm really here now. Will you do me a favor, old man, and let me prove I won the bet? I need to prove I came. Ah, yes.' He seemed to have just thought of it, picking up a note card and an envelope. 'This proves it. Mr. Clarber, thank you a thousand times!' Before I could say anything, he ran out.\" [1277] The inspector listened in astonishment to this extraordinary story, but as soon as Clarber's lips closed, he roared, \"A bunch of idiots! You let him go? Why -\"\n[1278] \"That's how we got the note,\" Peachins said slowly. [1279] \"Dear,\" Ren's voice was low. \"Unless necessary, let's not waste Clarber's time anymore. Clarber, can you describe this peculiar guest?\" [1280] \"Of course. Tall, slender, middle-aged, very British-looking.\" [1281] \"Good heavens!\" the inspector's voice was hoarse. \"Pett, this is\u2014\"\n[1282] \"Inspector, please.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nRowe, who had been idly watching Pechins doodle, stood up from his chair, and Pechins and Lane looked at him curiously. [2060] \"What are you writing?\" the young man asked sharply. [2061] \"What?\" Percy blinked. \"Just the symbol 3HS wM that's been driving us crazy!\" [2062] \"Hurrah!\" Roe exclaimed, jumping up and his eyes shining. \"I know! I know! It's so simple and childish!\" [2063] Jules Reine stood up, walked to the table, and his face was illuminated by the light. He said, \"You finally figured it out, Percy. When we sat in your father's office and he opened the Saxon letter to see what was written inside, I saw it and understood it too. Gordon, tell her.\" [2064] \"I don't understand what you two are talking about,\" Percy complained. [2065] \"How was I sitting when you wrote the symbol?\" Roe asked. [2066] \"Right in front of the table, facing me!\" [2067] \"Absolutely right. In other words, I saw this symbol just like Mr. Renet saw it when the commissioner opened the original letter. He saw what I saw - I saw it backwards!\" [2068] Percy gasped. She picked up the paper and turned it over. The symbol now looked like this:\n[2069] She slowly repeated, \"Wm She.\" Each letter seemed to have a different taste in her mouth, \"This looks like a signature. W-m...William...\" - both men looked at her intently - \"...William Shakespeare (1)!\" She shouted, jumping up from her chair, \"William Shakespeare!\" [2070] After a while, Percy sat down on the carpet next to the old gentleman's feet, and his pale, slender fingers played with her hair. Rowley relaxed comfortably in front of them. [2071] \"Ever since then, I've been thinking about this over and over in my mind,\" Reen explained wearily, \"When I analyzed it, it seemed quite clear. Dr. All\u00e8s was not imitating Shakespeare's signature; this copy should be the handwriting of the Elizabethan era. He simply wrote it in his own way - perhaps thinking he had written it clearly - the uppercase letters in this unusual Shakespeare signature. Unusual because of the lowercase m and the handwritten e. But why is H capitalized? It could be All\u00e8s' peculiar behavior, but it doesn't matter. What matters is that -\"\n[2072] \"It's one of Shakespeare's signatures,\" Rowley said slowly, \"It's quite strange!\" [2073] Reen sighed. \"Gordon, you know better than me that there are only six genuine Shakespeare signatures in the world.\" [2074] \"Strangely enough,\" the young man said, \"one of them is written as Will Shak'P'.\" [2075] \"Yes! There are also some so-called 'suspicious' signatures, one of which has a spelling like All\u00e8s' symbol - a capital W, a lowercase m connected to the top of the W, then a capital S, a lowercase h, and a lowercase e that is also connected.\" [2076] \"Is it like the old English script of 'ye'?\" Percy asked. [2077] \"You got it right. This suspicious signature appears in the Metamorphoses of Ovid (2) by Aldine, which is now housed in the Bodleian Library at Oxford.\" [2078] \"I saw it when I was in England,\" Rowe said. [2079] The old man continued, \"I checked with the Bodleian Library, and the book of Ovid is still there. I thought the whole thing might be related to the theft of that book. Of course, that's a ridiculous idea.\" [2080] \"You mean -\" Percy's tone was one of awe, \"that all these thefts, doubts, and everything else are related to the discovery of Shakespeare's seventh true signature?\" [2081] \"It seems so, doesn't it?\" Rowe said with a bitter smile. \"I've wasted my youth here. Ha, ha! I've never come across anything as momentous as this while going through Elizabethan records.\" [2082] \"What else could it be?\" Raine whispered. \"If the secret is really worth a million, Dr. All\u00e8s had reason to believe the signature was genuine. Otherwise, how could it be worth a million? Ha, it's a very interesting question.\" [2083] The young man murmured softly, \"If it has incalculable historical and literary value, it is priceless in itself.\" [2084] \"I read somewhere that a completely authentic seventh signature of Shakespeare found at an auction was worth a million pounds. I don't know if it's pounds or dollars! But the signature must have a purpose; it is usually associated with some document.\" [2085] \"The paper in the book!\" Percy exclaimed. [2086] \"Shh, Petty. It's not necessarily so,\" Rowe pondered. \"Six real signatures, of course, are associated with certain documents: one on a legal document where the old man was involved in a lawsuit; one on a contract signed when buying a house around 1612; another when paying for the same house; and the last three on his three wills. But it could also be on the back of a book's endpapers, you know.\" [2087] \"I doubt it,\" Raine shook his head. \"As Percy has already noticed, the seventh signature might be on a document - a transaction document, a lease - where the document itself has little historical value? Hmm, maybe\u2026\"\n[2088] \"The value can't be small,\" Rowe objected. \"If it's a transaction document or a lease, it could be of great importance, possibly explaining where Shakespeare was at some point - to clarify many things.\" [2089] \"Yes, yes. I said the value was small from a human perspective. But what if it's on a letter?\" Raine leaned forward, his fingers tightening in Percy's hair, making her almost cry out, \"Think of the possibilities! A signed letter by the immortal Shakespeare!\" [2090] \"I was thinking -\" Rowe muttered. \"This is too much. Who is it for? What does it say? Autobiographical data. A genuine Shakespeare manuscript.\" [2091] \"Of course, it's within the realm of possibility,\" the old gentleman's voice sounded slightly strained, \"If the signature appears at the end of the letter, the letter would be more valuable than the signature! No wonder the scholars are constantly scheming. It's like - oh, finding the actual manuscript of Saint Paul's letters!\" [2092] \"The document was in the 1599 Jagger,\" Perkins whispered hoarsely, \"Dr. Aless discovered the other two existing 1599 Jagger volumes and couldn't find anything, so he resorted to obtaining the third one, which belonged to the collection of Saxen. And he found it! This - is it possible...\"\n[2093] \"It seems so,\" Rowe smiled, \"He found it, lucky man!\" [2094] \"But now it's stolen. Oh, dear, I'd bet it was in Dr. Aless' cabinet.\" [2095] \"It's quite possible,\" said Reane. \"One more thing. I found out that the stolen and returned third book was actually bought by Samuel Saxen from Sir Humphrey, a collector from England.\" [2096] \"The same person?\" Percy was startled. [2097] \"The same.\" Raine shrugged, \"Sir Humphrey died just a few weeks ago. No, no. Don't be alarmed. His death was natural, no foul play. As usual, God was the executioner. He was eighty-nine and died of pneumonia complications. My contact on the other side told me that the Jager Saxon bought from Sir Humphrey was the one that caused all the trouble, and it had belonged to the Humphrey family since the Elizabethan era. Sir Humphrey was the last surviving member of the family, with no heirs.\" [2098] \"He couldn't have known the document was hidden in the book's cover,\" Rowe said, \"otherwise he wouldn't have sold it.\" [2099] \"Of course not. In fact, several generations of the Humphrey family didn't know or suspect the existence of the document.\" [2100] Percy asked, \"But why was the document hidden in the book cover? Who did it?\" [2101] Rine sighed, \"That's a question. I think the document has been hidden there for hundreds of years, probably written for a contemporary, who knows? But the document needed to be hidden, and I believe it has a special value or meaning in itself.\" [2102] Old Quincy slipped into the study, his wrinkled face resembling a leaf, with no part of it untouched by wrinkles. He tugged at the owner's sleeve and complained, \"Mr. Jury, there's a man named Bowlin, a police officer from Tarrytown.\" [2103] Rine frowned, \"You old knave! What did you say?\" [2104] \"He called. He told me that just an hour ago, at seven o'clock\" - the study clock pointed to seven - \"Aless's house was mysteriously blown up!\" [2105] --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- -