. The house collapsed on top of the cellar, and smoke still rose from the ash-covered ground. State troopers pushed their way through the curious crowd gathering around. The firefighters of Tarrytown had brought the fire under control and were now focusing on preventing the fire from spreading to the dry wood. The water supply for the fire engines was insufficient, so additional water tanks had to be brought in from Tarrytown and Ewington. [2112] Officer Pauling met Perkins, Rowe, and Rayne at the edge of the open space. His ruddy face was covered in soot, and he was panting heavily. He roared, \"Damn it! My two men are badly injured. Luckily, no one was inside when it happened. It exploded at six o'clock.\" [2113] \"No warning?\" Rayne asked, showing signs of excitement, \"I don't think it was a bomb dropped from a plane, right?\" [2114] \"Impossible. There hasn't been a single plane flying overhead all day. My two men said that no one came near the house for an hour or two after we left.\" [2115] \"So it must have been a bomb placed inside the house beforehand,\" Rowe said gloomily. \"Thank goodness it didn't go off earlier. What a close call!\" [2116] \"We could have been here when it exploded\u2014\" Perkins' face turned pale, \"This\u2014this is hard to believe. A bomb!\" [2117] \"It could have been in the cellar,\" Lane said indifferently, \"We didn't search there this afternoon. What an idiotic oversight!\" [2118] \"The cellar\u2014I suppose that's where it was,\" Bowlin grumbled, \"Well, my men need to be taken to the hospital. I'll go check on them. They were almost blown to pieces. We'll come back tomorrow when the fire is completely out.\" [2119] The three sat quietly in the old gentleman's car on the way back to Hamlet Manor, lost in their own thoughts. Rowe seemed particularly absorbed, with his fingers on his lower lip and his gaze fixed on the sky. [2120] \"You know,\" Rowe suddenly spoke up, \"I was thinking\u2014\"\n[2121] \"What?\" Perkins said. [2122] \"It seems like there's a group of people involved in this. Without a doubt, that Shakespeare document, whatever it is, must be the key. I think Dr. Alessi found it in the Jagger of 1599 that he stole from the museum, making him one of the main players. The other one is the guy who was waving an axe around last night, if he wasn't after the document, what was he? That's point two. Another person arrived after the ax-wielder, and he didn't close the secret cabinet door, that's point three. Now there's this explosion, someone planted a bomb, that's point four, God, that's enough to give you a headache.\" [2123] \"Maybe not.\" Perkins argued, \"The main players you mentioned - you're so technical - could be the same person. The second person to enter the house could have been Dr. Alessi, making the number three. The ax-wielder could have been the one who planted the bomb, leaving only two - Gordon, I'm going on like this, but there's one thing that's important. Now that I have time to think about this awful explosion, I have a very strange idea.\" [2124] The fog in Reine's eyes cleared, replaced by a look of curiosity. [2125] \"We've been assuming that the person chasing the document wanted the document itself - stealing, owning, preserving it, all for the money - a common criminal motive.\" [2126] Rowe chuckled. \"Patty, you talk fresh! Of course, the motivation for digging up something valuable is usually like that, and that's a normal explanation.\" [2127] Pechin sighed. \"Maybe I'm crazy, but I can't help thinking that if the bomb was placed last night, the person who put it there probably knew the document was in the house!\" [2128] The old gentleman blinked. \"Is that so, Pechin?\" [2129] \"Oh, I think it's crazy, but we're dealing with extreme acts of violence - gunshots, theft, explosions... Only Maxwell was in the house, so the person who set the bomb must have known that. If you think the target of that bomb was the harmless old servant, that's absurd. If it wasn't him, who or what was it? We always thought the person or people chasing the document would keep it. Tell you, someone's goal was to destroy it!\" [2130] Rowe paused for a moment, then threw his head back and bit his teeth. \"Oh, Patty, you're my nemesis. When it comes to women's arguments...\" He rubbed his eyes. \"What kind of bad guy wants to destroy a document with such a historical and monetary value? Who's so crazy!\" [2131] Pensin blushed. \"I think you're annoying.\" [2132] \"Gordon, Pensin's reasoning is very logical.\" Ryan quickly chimed in, \"Child, you can't outsmart this lady. I think if the document had only Shakespeare's signature, only a madman would destroy it. But if there's more to it than just the signature, the person who put the bomb definitely doesn't want the public to know what's on it, whatever it is.\" [2133] \"Well, what do you think, self-proclaimed genius?\" Pensin said. [2134] \"But to destroy -\" Luo Wei frowned, \"I can't imagine what secret Shakespeare would write that would make people in the 20th century go to great lengths to cover up. What is it? It doesn't make sense!\" [2135] \"That's the point.\" Ryan said coldly, \"What is it? If you know - as for whether it makes sense or not, that's another matter.\" [2136] If asked about Pechin, she might say that after this day, nothing would surprise her anymore. Looking back, the day started with a strange phone call, followed by a series of strange events \u2013 an old man attacked, a house mysteriously destroyed, and ended with a terrible explosion. Little did she know, there were still things waiting for her, Roe, and Raine at Hamlet Manor. [2137] As the sky darkened, the drawbridge was lit up, and the old face of Quincy shone brightly in front of the ancient lanterns. [2138] \"Mr. Jules!\" he called. \"Is anyone hurt?\" [2139] \"Not seriously, Quincy. What's going on?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe hid himself in the comfortable Hamlet Castle for nine days, remaining in a monastic silence. [1922] However, there was a minor incident this week. For example, the Inspector's office received two letters. One was from Dr. Leo Shilling, the Chief Medical Examiner of New York County, who made Manhattan's criminals tremble. From a medical perspective, the letters 3HS wM contained no chemical meaning. Initially, he wanted to divide the symbols into several parts. 3HS could represent three parts of hydrogen and sulfur, but there was no such chemical compound, as a hydrogen atom has stubbornly refused to bond with a sulfur atom since the beginning of time. As for the lowercase w, there could be different chemical interpretations, Dr. Shilling continued, such as watt being an electrical term and wolframite being a rare mineral. The uppercase M generally represents a metal. If w represented wolframite, then M and w might be related. The medical expert's conclusion was: \"In my opinion, the symbol composed of numbers and capital and lowercase letters is pure nonsense and has no scientific significance.\" [1923] The second letter was from Group Captain Sheff of the Washington Intelligence Bureau. [1924] Group Captain Sheff apologized for taking so long to reply to the unusual question from Inspector Sam. He had been very busy and may not have studied the symbol properly, but in his opinion, the symbol \"if it is a code, is a fairy tale.\" If it were a code, he did not believe it could be deciphered. If it had any meaning, it would require each letter to have a preset secret meaning. Experts might spend months trying to find a rule or method for deciphering it, only to end up without success. [1925] Percy was almost in tears, spending many nights studying the strange symbol in secret. Rowe tried to comfort her, but it was of no help, and his luck was not good either. [1926] Other reports came in, all without progress. One was a confidential report from Inspector Cagney: Dr. Hamnett Sedgwick arrived in New York from the Wester ship on May 22nd and did not officially appear at the British Museum until May 29th. During this period, the bureau's agents found no trace of him. Further investigation of the hotel where the Britisher stayed revealed that Dr. Sedgwick had stayed at the Seneca Hotel on May 29th. He had a lot of luggage at the time. He is now still staying at Seneca, a quiet middle-aged British man who usually dines alone in the \"Hunting Hall\" of the hotel. If he is in the hotel in the afternoon, he will call for afternoon tea at 4 o'clock and enjoy it alone in his room. [1927] The unfortunate Irish guard, Donohue, is still missing, and his fate remains a mystery. Dr. Ales also disappeared. [1928] Mr. Vera, the Italian, also reported to the authorities and received the appropriate punishment. One afternoon, Inspector Rowe explained to Gordon Rowe - obviously Rowe's opinion of the young man had changed greatly after being shot by the masked man and finding the false mustache - that Vera had left the room to find the phone when he was caught in the museum. Yes, it was probably Mr. Philo Vance's suggestion. Anyway, the purpose of the phone call was to have someone continue to track the cunning Vera after the inspector had finished with him. The person in charge of tracking was Gross, who had always worked for Sam Detective Agency. Gross silently followed the group out of the British Museum, to Dr. Ales' house in Tarrytown, waited quietly outside, and then skillfully followed the Italian man when the group came out. However, Gross's report was also unremarkable, as the thief had apparently given up investigating the \"million-dollar secret.\" [1929] Dr. Sedgwick kept coming and going in the museum. Dr. Jotter did the same. Kraab searched through his books at the Saxon manor. Mrs. Saxon was sweating and getting fat in the June heat, preparing to go to the villa in France... Everyone was behaving normally. Everyone was as innocent as Percy's blue eyes. As Inspector Sam said to his colleagues after briefly investigating the jewelry case, \"This is probably the most difficult and complicated case I've ever encountered.\" [1930] Maxwell should still be guarding Dr. Ales' house alone. [1931] The phone rang. [1932] The phone rang on the first day of July, a hot Monday morning. The inspector had been out of town for two days, pursuing the recent mysterious case. Gordon Rowe slept peacefully in his hotel room \u2013 he had packed his meager belongings and left the Saxon house, telling Perkins that he was \"never coming back.\" Miss Broadie sat mournfully in the reception area of the inspector's office, while Perkins sat at the inspector's desk, frowning at his father's note, which bore the postmark \"Braf City Council, Iowa.\" [1933] Miss Broadie's cry carried through the open door: \"Sam Miss, could you please answer the phone? I don't understand what he's saying. He sounds drunk.\" [1934] \"Oh, dear,\" Perkins sighed, reaching for the phone. Miss Broadie could be quite a bother sometimes. [1935] \"Hello?\" she said lazily, then stiffened, as if struck by electricity. [1936] The voice on the other end was undoubtedly that of Mr. Maxwell. But what a sad voice it was! Choked with tears, weak and panicked - he stammered, and Percy could only make out a few words: \"Help - house - terrible - Inspector Sam, come quick -\" The syllables were muddled and hard to make out. [1937] \"Maxwell!\" Percy exclaimed, \"What's happened? Has Dr. Allison returned?\" [1938] The old man's voice, though weak, was clear: \"No. Come quickly!\" Then there was silence on the line, as if something heavy had fallen. Percy stared at the phone, then yelled into it. There was no response. \"Maxwell!\" It was clear that poor Mr. Maxwell could not hear or respond. [1939] Percy rushed to the front office, put on his hat. \"Miss Brewster, please call Mr. Quixi... Mr. Quixi! This is Percy. Is Mr. Raine there?\" But Mr. Quixi could not help, he said that Mr. Raine was somewhere on the estate, but he did not know where; but he would find the master and pass on Percy's message to hurry to the Allisons'... Percy dialed Gordon Roxean's new number. [1940] \"Oh, dear, Percy. It sounds serious. Wait, I'll sober up... Have you called the police?\" [1941] \"Police? What police?\" [1942] \"Miss, the police in Therry Town! Percy, my dear, your wit this morning seems to have taken a leave of absence. Do a good deed and find someone to help that old man!\" [1943] \"Oh, Gordon. I'm so sorry, I'm so stupid. I should have thought of it sooner. I'll notify them right away. I'll come and pick you up in twenty minutes.\" [1944] \"Be brave, Darling!\" [1945] But when Percy called the Therry Town police station, the duty officer \u2013 a man named Bolling \u2013 was not available, and a tired assistant seemed to misunderstand the urgency of the situation, finally agreeing to send someone to check on it. [1946] The more urgent the situation became, the paler Perkins' lips grew. [1947] \"I'm going out,\" she announced to Miss Brewster tragically. \"Oh, what a mess! I think poor Maxwell is lying in a pool of blood. Goodbye!\" [1948] Perkins pulled up the sports car to the mouth of the alley. Gordon Roe stood up and squinted down the road. [1949] \"I think that's Lien's car.\" [1950] A long black sedan sped towards them. It screeched to a halt in front of them, and both of them breathed a sigh of relief. The reckless driver was DeRome. The rear door opened, and Rene's tall figure leaped out lightly. [1951] \"Oh, I'm terribly sorry, children,\" he called out. \"You've just arrived? I went swimming, and poor Quincey couldn't find me. Have you called the police?\" [1952] \"They should be here soon,\" Percy swallowed and said. [1953] \"No,\" the elderly gentleman whispered, eyeing the pebbles on the path. \"It rained heavily last night, and the pebbles are still black, and the ground is still soft, with no tire marks... for some reason, they haven't arrived yet. Let's go and check ourselves. Gordon, I see your arm has healed... dear, let's go! Be careful, we don't know what we'll encounter!\" [1954] He got back into the car. Percy drove the sports car into the alley, with Dromio following in the large vehicle. The trees hung low, like a canopy. The morning rain had washed the pebbles on the road, making them look like pristine paper. The young couple remained silent, with Percy focusing on the narrow path, and Roe gazing ahead. They didn't know what to be wary of. If someone with a weapon emerged from the bushes or a group of people blocked the road with machine guns, they wouldn't be prepared. The two cars moved forward, making a squeaking sound, but nothing happened. [1955] When they reached the entrance to the narrow lane leading to the Allis family's house, Percy stopped the car. Lane got out of the back seat. The three of them formed a small team. The countryside was filled with the usual sounds of summer, making them feel joyful. However, as they looked around, they couldn't see any signs of human presence. They decided to leave the cars in the alley, with Dromio watching over them, and walk forward. [1956] They cautiously walked down the lane, with Rowe leading the way, Rayne following, and Percy nervously sandwiched between them. The trees became sparser, and they could see the open space in front of the house. The house was very desolate, with the front door closed, the shutters on the windows closed, and the garage door closed, with no apparent abnormalities. [1957] \"Where's Maxwell?\" Percy whispered. [1958] \"Let's go inside and take a look. I don't like this,\" Rowe said worriedly, \"Percy, stay close to me. We don't know what might happen.\" [1959] They quickly crossed the open space and climbed the wobbly stairs to the porch. Rowe pounded heavily on the thick door, ove