disappeared Saturday!\" Rowe countered. [2157] \"I know. It's just strange. I don't know what to do. Should I call the police or\u2026 I tried to reach your father, Miss Samuels, but the office secretary said\u2026\"\n[2158] \"First Donoho, then Dr. Aless, and now Sedra,\" Percy lamented. \"These people are disappearing one by one! It's \u2013 it's unacceptable!\" [2159] \"Unless Sedra is Aless,\" Rowe said. [2160] Dr. Jotter ran his fingers through his hair. \"Oh, my God!\" [2161] Pinchot frowned. \"I don't think that necessarily means Dr. Aless is Sedra, or that he escaped with the file.\" [2162] \"Dear Miss Sampson, the hotel staff said all of his things were still in the room. I don't think a person running away would do that. What file are you talking about\u2014\"\n[2163] Raine looked exhausted, with deep circles under his eyes and skin that looked like crumpled parchment. He wearily shook his head: \"These conjectures won't get us anywhere. It's a surprising turn of events\u2026 The only thing I can suggest is to find out what happened to Sedra.\" [2164] Pinchot and Roebling arrived in town late at night. They parked their car outside the Seneca Hotel and went inside to find the manager. After some hesitation, they were granted permission to look at Dr. Sedra's room. The room seemed tidy, with British-cut clothes neatly hanging in the closet, freshly laundered sheets and pillowcases in the dresser, and three unopened suitcases and bags. The manager seemed reluctant to involve the police, glancing at Pinchot's credentials\u2014which, of course, were the detective's\u2014and reluctantly allowing the search of the room. [2165] The luggage and clothes were all British. There were some letters in the room, with postmarks from \"London\" and addressed to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, apparently from former British colleagues. The passport and visa were in order, issued to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, with a familiar-looking photo attached. [2166] \"It's Sedra, no doubt,\" Rowe said grimly. \"I'm starting to get paranoid about this. There's no sign here that this person wanted to flee the country.\" [2167] \"Ugh!\" Peachins muttered under his breath. \"Gordon, take me home, and\u2014and, kiss me!\" [2168] Chapter 25\n[2169] Murder\n[2170] The sun shone brightly. The fire had been extinguished, and the smoke had dispersed with the night, leaving only burnt-out ashes, piles of rubble, and charred trees to bear witness to the explosion of the night before. Firefighters and police were busy digging through the rubble. A dark-skinned man calmly directed the entire operation, his eyes sharp and eager to clear away the debris so he could descend into the cellar to see what was inside. [2171] They stood by the edge of the woods, the warm morning breeze rustling their clothes. Pauling watched the workers with a cold eye. [2172] \"See that guy with the hawk eyes? He's an explosives expert. I think since he's involved, he should do a good job! I want to see how this strange thing happened.\" [2173] \"You mean he'll find something in that pile of garbage?\" Roeve asked. [2174] \"That's why he's here.\" [2175] The workers made rapid progress, quickly clearing the debris blocking the cave. They shoveled the garbage to thirty feet away, hand over hand. Once the cellar was almost clear enough to descend, the silent man crawled into the hole and disappeared. Ten minutes later, he reappeared, looking around as if measuring the extent of the explosion, and then disappeared again, this time into the woods. He reappeared again and descended into the cellar. The third time he emerged, he wore a satisfied expression, holding in his hands a jumbled mess of iron pieces, rubber, glass, and wires. [2176] \"How's it going?\" Pauling asked. [2177] \"Sir, the evidence is here.\" The explosives expert said, picking up a fragment of a clock-like instrument, \"A time bomb.\" [2178] \"Ah!\" Mr. Philo Vance said. [2179] \"Primitive, homemade. Set to go off at six o'clock with yellow dynamite.\" [2180] Percy, Roe, and Vance all thought the same thing, but Vance was the first to ask: \"When was the bomb placed?\" [2181] \"At six o'clock on Sunday evening - if the bomb went off last night at six o'clock. It's a twenty-four-hour timer.\" [2182] \"Six o'clock on Sunday evening.\" Perkins repeated slowly, \"So it was put on before Maxwell was attacked on Sunday night!\" [2183] \"You're right, Perri.\" Rowe said, \"If the person who put the bomb knew there were documents in the house, his purpose was to destroy the documents, so he knew the stuff was in the house but didn't know exactly where. It's difficult -\"\n[2184] \"The epicenter of the explosion was the cellar.\" The expert spat on the charred rock. [2185] Reine let out another \"Ah.\" [2186] \"The second visitor \u2013 the one who took the documents out of the secret cabinet \u2013\" Perkins looked pointedly at Reen, \"could not have been the one who planted the bomb. It's obvious. The second visitor knew where the documents were; the bomber didn't, as you said, Gordon\u2026\" Perkins' words were interrupted by a coarse roar, coming from the workers digging through the cellar debris. They all turned around. [2187] \"What is it?\" Bowlin called, running over quickly. [2188] Three people were bending over something, their heads almost touching. One of them turned around, pale and trembling. He managed to speak: \"Sir, there's a body here. From the look on his face, he was \u2013 murdered.\" [2189] The young man ran towards the edge of the house foundation, stepping on black ash. Reen followed slowly, his face pale and anxious. [2190] Roarke looked at the scene in front of him and roughly pushed Perkins aside. \"Petty, get away from here,\" he said hoarsely. \"You'd better go stand under that tree over there. This \u2013 it's not good.\" [2191] \"Oh,\" Perkins said. Her nostrils flared, and she silently obeyed his request. [2192] The men all widened their eyes and focused intently on the deep hole. A young, red-faced policeman crawled out from the corner of the cellar, bent over, and began to shake and vomit... The body was badly burned, almost unrecognizable - one leg and one arm were missing, and the clothes were completely burnt. [2193] \"How do you know he was murdered?\" Reen asked sternly. [2194] An older policeman's mouth twisted: \"He's not completely burnt, I can see the hole.\" [2195] \"Hole?\" Rowe croaked. [2196] The man sighed. \"Three holes. In his stomach, clean and neat. Those are bullet holes, sir, don't forget.\" [2197] Three hours later, Raine, Detective Bolling, Peachins, and the young Roe sat silently in the office of the Prosecutor of Tarrytown. Someone had called to dispatch a car to take the body to the morgue in Tarrytown. Apart from the usual procedures of collecting the remains and their belongings, Bolling ordered no one to touch the body. The officers searched for fragments of the clothing, hoping to find buttons that could provide clues to the victim's identity. However, the explosion had damaged the body so much that the officer soon gave up searching. The explosives expert happily said that the body was not turned into atoms, a miracle. [2198] They sat at the prosecutor's desk, staring at the items on the table. This was the only item taken from under the body that could be used as a clue \u2013 a cheap British-made watch with a leather strap. If traced back to its source, it would be a waste of time. The watch case was almost gone, leaving only a small triangular piece of glass. The synthetic metal used to make the watch, despite its surface turning gray-black, was not damaged by the explosion. There was one strange thing: the hands pointed to 12:26; the face of the watch had a deep cut that went over the number \"10\" and extended to the edge of the watch. [2199] \"That's odd,\" the prosecutor, a young man with worried eyes, said. \"Bolling, didn't you tell me the body was found face down, with the arm wearing this watch pressed under the stomach?\" [2200] \"Yes.\" [2201] \"Then the cut on the case of the watch was not left by the explosion.\" [2202] \"There are other suspicious things,\" Percy said. \"If the watch stopped because of the explosion, the pointer should have stopped at six o'clock, but it didn't.\" [2203] The prosecutor looked at her approvingly. \"Right! I didn't even think of it myself. You're Sam Chief's daughter, aren't you?\" [2204] The coroner rushed in - a short, bald man with a pink face and a soft chin - \"Hello, hello! I just finished checking the mess inside the body.\" \"He was murdered, right?\" Rowe asked eagerly. [2205] \"Yes.\" [2206] \"Yes, indeed. Of course, it's difficult to make a definite diagnosis based on the condition of the body, but I estimate that he died more than thirty-six hours ago, so he must have died around midnight on Sunday.\" [2207] \"Midnight on Sunday!\" Percy glared at Roe, who glared back at her. Mr. Jury Ring shuddered. [2208] \"This is consistent with the time on the watch,\" the prosecutor concluded. \"Twelve o'clock - twenty - six minutes. The watch must have stopped at the time of the murder. He was killed at zero point twenty-six on Monday.\" [2209] The little bald man continued, \"He was shot from the front, at close range. Three bullets.\" He dropped three deformed bullets on the table. \"The slash on the case is strange. There is also a deep cut on the wrist. The beginning of the cut on the wrist is exactly where the slash on the case stops.\" [2210] \"In other words,\" Roe asked, \"you think the same attack caused the cuts on both the wrist and the case?\" [2211] \"That's the standard answer!\" [2212] \"This is our axe-wielder,\" Roe's eyes blazed with anger, \"or at least the person who used the axe... Doctor, could these cuts have been made by an axe?\" [2213] \"Yes, it couldn't have been a knife. The blade of the weapon must have been wide and had a handle.\" [2214] \"That settles it,\" Pauling said. \"Someone used an axe on this guy, hitting his wrist, smashing his watch, stopping it, and injuring his wrist. And, I think, the killer fed him lead pellets.\" [2215] \"There's one more thing.\" The coroner pulled a small key wrapped in a tissue from his pocket. \"Pauling, your men just brought it over, they found it in a fragment of a pants pocket near the body. Someone has identified it\u2014\"\n[2216] \"Maxwell?\" [2217] \"The caretaker of the house? Yes, Maxwell identified this as the original key to the front door.\" [2218] \"Original!\" The two young men exclaimed in unison. [2219] \"Strange.\" Pauling said. \"Wait a minute!\" He picked up the prosecutor's phone and dialed the headquarters in Tarrytown. He spoke briefly to someone and then hung up the phone, saying, \"Yes. My men told me this is Dr. Alessi's key. The key taken by the masked man who tied Maxwell up in the garage that night was a copy.\" [2220] \"The only original?\" Percy gasped. [2221] \"That's what Maxwell said.\" [2222] \"So, there's nothing to doubt,\" the prosecutor sighed contentedly. \"The body must be that of Dr. Allais.\" [2223] \"Really?\" Reine murmured. [2224] \"Don't you think so?\" [2225] \"Dear sir, a key does not necessarily represent an owner. However, I suppose that logically, it makes sense.\" [2226] \"Well, I'm busy.\" The forensic expert said, \"And there's one more thing. I think you need the deceased's physical features. He's five feet eleven inches tall, has light yellow or golden hair, weighs about one hundred fifty-five pounds, and is between forty-five and fifty-five years old. I couldn't find any other identifying marks.\" [2227] \"Sedra.\" Percy whispered. [2228] \"Bingo.\" Roe impatiently said, \"A British doctor named Sedra was involved in this case, and he disappeared from his New York hotel on Saturday. This description fits him perfectly.\" [2229] \"You don't mean that guy!\" Paul shouted. [2230] \"I do mean him. However, the identification seems a bit vague. This Sedra person is also suspected to be Dr. Alessi\u2014\"\n[2231] \"So that's it!\" Bauer exclaimed hopefully. \"Don't forget, we carried the body with Dr. Aless' key. If Sedra is Aless, then everything falls into place.\" [2232] \"I'm not so sure,\" Rowe stammered. \"There are actually two possibilities. We're arguing over here because we haven't analyzed it thoroughly enough. The first possibility is that Sedra and Aless are the same person, just like you said, Mr. Bauer. In this case, the body \u2013 the features match both of them \u2013 clears up the mystery of the two missing persons. But if Sedra and Aless are not the same person, then we can only conclude that they are 'physically similar.' We've been avoiding this conclusion because it seems \u2013 uh \u2013 like a clich\u00e9 from a novel, but you can't ignore it.\" [2233] Reen remained silent. [2234] \"Well,\" Bauer stood up, \"maybe these ramblings can solve your problem. I just want to know one thing: who is this corpse? Dr. Aless? Or that damn Britisher Sedra?\" [2235] Two important things happened on Tuesday morning. Chief Sam returned victorious from Ohio, having apprehended the jewel thief who was safely behind bars in an iron window; and the mystery of the \"physically similar\" was solved. [2236] Chapter 26\n[2237] Resurrection\n[2238] The next morning, the two old men and two young people sat under the large oak tree in the peaceful, serene garden of Reine. \"We're here again because...\" the officer cheerfully told Reine, \"Petty told me she and this young man have practically lived here! We have some interesting news to tell you.\" [2239] \"News?\" the old gentleman shrugged, looking tired and weary, then he smiled weakly, a little of his old-time pleasantness creeping into his voice, \"\u2018Fill me with your rich voice, and let all my senses be fed, / Till I am as a seagull come to cormorant's nest on the head.\u2019 (1) I believe the harvest is bountiful.\" [2240] The officer grinned, in a very good mood. \"Judge for yourself!\" he reached into his pocket and pulled out an envelope, \"This morning I received an unexpected telegram from the old man Truncus.\" [2241] The telegram read:\n[2242] Further investigation into Hamnet Sedley reveals an interesting fact. The previous telegram informed you that Hamnet's brother William was missing, and it has now been discovered that William and Hamnet are twin brothers. William has arrived in America, arriving in New York from Bordeaux in late March. He is wanted by the Bordeaux police for illegal entry and malicious destruction of the private library of a French book collector, with the intent to steal rare books. The Frenchman was brutally beaten after discovering that William had damaged the cover of a 1599 edition of Shakespeare's \"The Passionate Pilgrim.\" William's behavior is strange, and he is well-off, like Hamnet, a book collector, and disappeared from Britain three years ago under the pseudonym of Dr. Allis. He served as an expert in rare book auctions, purchasing books for millionaire collectors. His best customer was the recently deceased Sir Humphrey. Both William and Hamnet have no fingerprints on record, and they have no discernible features to consider, but they bear a striking resemblance to each other.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nGood on you, Gordon, kid. Congratulations. Alright, let's get on with it -\"\n[2591] \"Dad!\" Percy whispered. He stopped talking, and all the joyful expressions vanished from his face. Rowe held her weak hand, and she gently squeezed it back. [2592] \"Does he know everything? Really?\" [2593] \"Everything? Oh, Rain! He said so, Petty. He said so. He said so. He said so.\" He came over, wrapping his long arms around her like a monkey, \"What's the difference? What matters is that you're back, and that's all that matters to me.\" [2594] She gently pushed him away. \"No, there are some things\u2014\"\n[2595] Sam furrowed his brow. \"He told me to let you know as soon as you got back. I'd better make a call\u2026\"\n[2596] \"Really?\" - The haggard look on Peachins' face disappeared, and her eyes suddenly became feverish. The two men stared at her, thinking she was crazy\u2014\"No, I tell you, it's best if we tell him ourselves. Oh, I'm such a stupid, stubborn, disgusting idiot!\" [2597] \"But, Petty\u2014\" Rowe protested. [2598] \"Go. I knew... Oh, we might be too late!\" She turned and ran out of the apartment. Rowe and Sam exchanged uneasy glances, then grabbed their hats and ran after her. [2599] They piled into the sports car and sped away. Rowe drove, and if he was a gentle bookworm under the streetlights, he was a demon behind the wheel. For a while\u2014until they left the city traffic behind\u2014they didn't speak. Rowe drove intently. Peachins was pale, with a strange, dreamy look in her eyes. Sam looked like a sphinx, with a face full of alertness. [2600] When the city was far behind, the broad road stretched out in front of them like a white ribbon, the Chief broke the silence: \"Peachins, tell us what's going on. Obviously, Rene is in trouble. I don't know you, so you should have told me earlier\u2014\"\n[2601] \"Yeah,\" her voice was hoarse, \"it's my fault... Dad, it's unfair not to let you know. And you, Gordon. It's important for both of you to know. Gordon, drive faster! There's... there's a disaster ahead!\" [2602] Roarke's lips tightened. The sports car sped forward like a frightened hare. [2603] \"In the end...,\" Perkins said, her nose twitching, \"you saw it too. We came to the conclusion that the victim and the killer were the Sedra brothers. We thought one of them killed the other in the house. But then things changed. Last week - at the museum - things changed. We found out that the dead in the rubble was Hamnet, and the survivor was his brother William, and William couldn't have been one of the two people who entered the house that night. You remember how I proved that - with the key. That means our reasoning doesn't hold up; we know the victim was Hamnet Sedra, but we don't know who was the first person to enter the house that night, the kidnapper of Maxwell, the axe-wielder... The thought of it scares me. I remember some forgotten things that happened or I saw, that didn't make sense at the time, but later became - like a lightning bolt - terrifyingly clear.\" [2604] She looked ahead at the road. \"The last key to the whole problem is to find out who was the first person to enter the house. What happened then? This person tied Maxwell up, stuffed his mouth, took Maxwell's duplicate key, and went back into the house. The door closed automatically behind him because it had a spring lock. He took a small axe from the wooden box in the kitchen and attacked the study. Obviously, in theory, the study was the most likely place to hide the files he was looking for. He didn't know where the files might be hidden in the study; he hacked everything ruthlessly, that's the evidence. First, he might have gone through all the books, guessing that the folder was in one of them. Not finding it, he hacked the furnishings of the house - the paneling, the floor, etc. At midnight - we knew from the clock's hands - he destroyed the clock, I guess he thought it might be hiding the files. He was clueless, he couldn't find the files in the study, nor anywhere else on the first floor. So he went upstairs to William Sedra's bedroom, because that was the second most likely place to hide something.\" [2605] \"We know all that, Petty.\" Sam looked at her strangely. [2606] \"Dad, don't worry... From the broken bedroom clock, we know he was in the bedroom at 12:24. Alright, according to Hamnet's watch, he was killed in the house at 12:26 - just two minutes after the axeman broke the clock in the upstairs bedroom. The question is: When did Hamnet enter the house? He had to open the door, walk into the study, see the mess, climb up to the hollow wall panel above the bookshelf, take out the document, climb down the ladder, possibly check the document, and then encounter the murderer, struggle, and be killed. Obviously, this whole process took more than two minutes! So Hamnet must have entered the house while the axeman was still inside.\" [2607] \"So what?\" The officer's voice grew stern. [2608] \"I'm about to get there,\" said Percy calmly. \"William Sedgwick said that Hamnet wanted to destroy the documents. Once Hamnet got the documents in the study, what would he do? He would immediately destroy them. How would he do it? Fire would be the quickest and most convenient method. He would have struck a match, holding the document in his hand, and started putting the paper on the flame.\u201d She sighed. \"Of course, this is only theoretical and not very meaningful, but it clears up one point. This explains the axe marks on Hamnet's wrist and watch! Because if just as Hamnet was about to bring the matchstick close to the document, he was caught by the axe-wielder - who wanted to save the document and not destroy it - in a state of panic, he would naturally attack Hamnet to prevent the document from being destroyed by fire. So he swung his axe at Hamnet's hand like lightning, cutting his wrist and watch, forcing the destroyer to let go of the document and the matchstick. Undoubtedly, Hamnet also resisted. In the struggle, the axe-wielder shot him. The whole struggle may have started in the study, where the axe-wielder put down the axe and slowly moved towards the corridor, where we found Hamnet's single-lens glasses, where he may have been shot... The axe-wielder dragged Hamnet's body down to the cellar, unaware of the bomb there, and then, if the document had not been destroyed before he swung his axe at Hamnet's wrist, he picked up the document and left the house. From the traces of hacking and struggling, the axe-wielder seemed to go to any lengths - physical combat, murder - to preserve the document.\" [2609] Hamlet Manor is situated on the cliff above, and Rowe is fully concentrated on driving along the steep road. As he skillfully wrestles with the winding road, Percy remains silent. Suddenly, the manor appears before them. The car passes through the odd little bridge, and the tires sing along the gravel path. [2610] Luo Wei furrowed his brows and asked, \"Even if all of these are true, Petty, I still don't understand what the outcome is. The traces of the killer are still as elusive as ever.\" [2611] Rowe frowns. \"Even if all this is true, Peri, I still don't understand what the result is. The killer's tracks are still as elusive as ever.\" [2612] \"You think so?\" Perkins exclaimed, closing her eyes and shivering slightly, like a child swallowing bitter medicine. \"This is all very clear, as clear as - original sin! This man's features - his features, Gordon - what happened in the house exposed his identity!\" [2613] The two men look at her in confusion. They are now passing through the gate and slowly descending the winding driveway. Quixi's small figure and leathery face emerge from the lilac bushes, squinting and then smiling, revealing countless wrinkles. He waves and jumps onto the road. [2614] Rowe stops the car. [2615] \"Quixi!\" Perkins' voice stiffens, standing slightly between the two men. \"How is Mr. Lane?\" [2616] \"Letter?\" Sam said, puzzled, \"Strange. Here you go.\" Quincey handed him an envelope, which he tore open. [2617] \"Letter?\" Peachins was equally bewildered, sitting back between the two men and staring at the blue sky, saying, \"Thank God, he's alright.\" [2618] The inspector had been quietly reading the letter when his eyebrows furrowed into a deep crease and he read aloud in a loud voice -\n[2619] Dear Inspector:\n[2620] I believe that Peachins has ended her terrifying experience and has returned home safely. I know that my \"special statement\" will safely bring her home. While you were waiting, you may have hoped to find some answers to the mysteries - that have troubled you in your investigation - to distract yourself. [2621] The main question, as Percy and Gordon pointed out, is why a sensible, intelligent, and learned man like Hamnet Sadler would want to destroy a manuscript written by the hand of Shakespeare himself, which is so rare and invaluable that it cannot be replaced? I have myself unraveled this mystery, and can now tell you the answer. [2622] This manuscript is a letter to the ancestor of Sir Humphrey, who was a close friend of the poet. The author - Shakespeare - not only tells him that he suspects himself of being slowly poisoned, but also mentions the name of the suspicious poisoner... This is a very strange world, Shakespeare accuses the person named Hamnet Sadler. Inspector, Hamnet Sadler is the direct ancestor of the Sadler brothers. [2623] Strange, isn't it? That's why a scholar, a learned man, a sincere and wise antiquarian, a proud American, would defy his instincts of education and science, and even destroy something that could become the most precious treasure in the world, to hide this from the world: Shakespeare - Carlyle (3) praised the poet as having \"the greatest wisdom\"; Ben Jonson said he was \"not only of the moment, but of the ages\" - a man who has been worshipped and revered by people for three hundred years, has been murdered by the ancestor of Hamnet Sadler; even more terrifying, the ancestor and the name are the same! Some people may find a touch of madness in his enthusiasm, while others refuse to believe it; but the pride of the ancestors and the aging are incurable diseases, burning in the cold flame... William was not infected by this disease, his scientific spirit overcame them. But he still couldn't avoid the secular entanglement, to seize the document for himself and not pass it on to posterity. The third person in this case, the main character who appeared only once in the murder of the night, was willing to sacrifice his life to preserve the document for the world. [2624] Tell Percy, Gordon, and anyone else interested - the truth will soon be revealed to the world, old friend - they don't have to worry about the safety of the document. I personally took care of it and sent it back to its rightful owner in Britain - it is legally British property and spiritually belongs to the whole world - because the legal owner, Sir Humphrey, is no longer alive, he has no heirs, and his property has been donated to the royal family. If I can protect this work, Inspector, I know my friend will always remember my kindness. Like the self-importance of the worldly, I will think that even in the last stage of my life, I can still do something for humanity. [2625] Percy and Gordon, forgive this old man for paying attention to your intimate relationship, I think you two will be very happy together. You have similar interests, equal talents, and are promising young people, I know you will respect each other. May God bless you. I haven't forgotten you. [2626] My dear Detective, I am old and tired, and it seems that there is nothing... I am about to leave - I think, to take a long rest. Because I leave without anyone by my side, and you don't know, so I say these beautiful words to say goodbye:\n[2627] They say he left peacefully, having done his duty, so may heaven be with him! [2628] Until we meet again...\n[2629] Philo Vance\n[2630] Detective, he wrinkled his nose. \"I don't understand...\"\n[2631] Rowe glanced around quickly, and the peaceful, gleaming towers of Hamlet Manor shone above the treetops. [2632] Percy's voice tightened: \"Quincannon, where is Mr. Rain?\" [2633] Quincannon's little frog eyes brightened. \"Sunbathing in the West Garden, Miss Sam. I'm sure he'll be surprised to see you, I know he wasn't expecting any guests today.\" [2634] The two men jumped out of the car, and Percy stiffly stepped onto the gravel path, walking between them. Quince quietly followed behind. Percy began to walk across the lush, green grass towards the West Garden. [2635] \"You know...\" her voice was barely audible, and they had to strain their ears, \"the axe man revealed himself. He didn't make a mistake, he knew he didn't make a mistake - fate made the mistake for him, fate disguised as a cheap alarm clock.\" [2636] \"Alarm clock?\" the policeman muttered. [2637] \"When we searched the study, we saw Maxwell's alarm clock on the mantelpiece, still set to the time. What does this mean? The alarm clock rang at the appointed time - midnight twelve - because we checked it the next morning after Maxwell set the time, that is, before noon twelve. Do you remember we searched it and the timer rod was pointing to 'open'. If we saw the timer rod set to 'open', then the alarm clock must have stopped ringing. What does it mean for the alarm clock to ring? If it stopped, we saw the timer rod still pointing to 'open', which means it must have rung until it stopped. If it was turned off when it rang, then the timer rod wouldn't be pointing to 'open', but to 'closed'. So, the alarm clock wasn't turned off. It rang and rang until the spring inside broke, and the timer rod still pointed to 'open...'\n[2638] \"But what does this mean, Petty?\" Luo Wei called out. [2639] \"This explains everything. We know the axeman was in the room at midnight, so when the alarm clock started ringing, he must have been there too. We know this from two things: Maxwell said he always kept the clocks in the house running on time, and the floor clock was broken at exactly twelve o'clock.\" [2640] Luo Wei took a step back, silent, pale-faced. [2641] \"Okay, I'm listening,\" the officer called nervously. \"But why didn't your axe murderer turn off the alarm when it rang? He must have been shocked. In any case, anyone sneaking around in someone else's house would jump up, turn it off, whether anyone heard it or not.\" [2642] They stopped under an old oak tree, and Percy absent-mindedly stroked the rough bark. \"That's right,\" she said softly. \"The fact is, even if he was in the same room, and every instinct would have urged him to turn off the alarm, he didn't do it.\" [2643] Sam grumbled, \"I don't get it. Let's go, Gordon.\" He walked past the tree, and the others followed slowly. Not far away, they saw Raine sitting quietly on a wooden bench, facing away from them. [2644] Percy made a sad choking sound, and the officer quickly turned around. Roche's eyes were blank, and he rushed forward, putting his hand on her waist. [2645] \"What's going on?\" the officer asked slowly. [2646] \"Dad, wait,\" Percy cried, tears streaming down her face. \"Wait. You don't understand. You still don't understand. The axe-wielder dragged Hamnet Sedley's body into the cellar without hearing the ticking of the bomb. Why did he have to chop open the wall panel? He was obviously looking for a hollow spot. What is the normal procedure when looking for a hollow spot? Tap lightly! Tap lightly! Dad! Why didn't he tap those wall panels?\" [2647] Sam looked at Percy, then at Gordon, then with a mix of bewilderment and unease. \"Why?\" [2648] Percy placed her trembling hand on his large one. \"Please. Look at him in front of you. The axe-wielder didn't turn off the alarm, didn't investigate the ticking of the bomb in the cellar, didn't tap the wall panels \u2013 Dad, the reason is the same. Oh, do you understand? I thought so much, and then suddenly realized. Such a terrible realization, I ran away blindly. I want to run away, anywhere...\"\n[2649] The little valley was silent. The officer's jaw sagged like a rusty gate, and his eyes were filled with terror after the realization. Rowe stood like a statue, his arms rigidly holding Percy's trembling body. Quince, who was trailing behind, suddenly let out a stifled scream and collapsed onto the grass. [2650] The officer stumbled forward, touching Raine's quiet shoulder. Percy turned around, burying her face in Rowe's coat, crying as if her heart had broken. [2651] The old gentleman's head dropped low to his chest, and he did not respond to Sam's touch. [2652] The inspector's large size and weight did not impede his agility, and he came round to the front of the chair and grasped Rayne's hand. [2653] His hand had gone cold, and an empty potion bottle slipped between pale fingers onto the grass.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."